I’m happy with the result so far.”
Former Masters winner Ian Woosnam and senior tournament winner Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark James and Mark McNulty will also join three players on the pitch.
Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance are among the six Scottish players on the pitch; the others are Andrew Oldcorn, Stephen McAllister, Bill Longmuir and Ross Drummond.
Entrance to the tournament is free of charge. Parking is £5 for a car with one passenger and £10 for a car with two or more passengers.
All profits will be donated to Prostate Cancer UK.
Poverty and Death in the Indonesian Land of Gold
When Bardina Degei cooks dinner, she doesn't use the stove.
In fact, it seldom uses the pelvis.
In its wooden shack, which is located in Paniai Enarotali in the Indonesian province of Papua, a woman usually simply throws a sweet potato - in the language of the local "note" - directly into the fire.
After half an o'clock, he exhorts the potato and enjoys it with unwashed hands.
Bardina sits on the muzzling ground - the house isn't equipped with furniture - where it also performs its daily work, such as washing clothes in turbid water from a nearby sworing swamp.
Kbelík in a room without a roof replaces the toilet.
As the youngest of her husband's four wives, she wasn't assigned any field for cultivation.
Polygamy is common here.
Work until late at night poses a danger: most men from the village are unemployed and often drank alcohol. In addition, there are soldiers in the village.
"In the afternoon after five o'clock, no one dares to roam around the village," Bardina says.
It's a rare glimpse into everyday life in the outcue of Papui, a former Dutch colony that was proclamated as part of Indonesia in 1969 following a controversy, when only 1026 elders were coerced into a public upbringing vulnerabilities in front of an occupying army.
The current movement protesting against the Dutch rules quickly reversed its wrath against the Yaktar government, which dominated the entire region, with the exception of foreign journalists or overseers of civilian rights.
In 2003 the province was officially divided into Papu and West Papu - independent Papua New Guinea occupied the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is such a distant and devastated city, which can be reached after a ninety-minute flight from Xisupura's provincial provincial to Nabire, then a five-hundred-hundred-hundred-shielding stomach.
Public transport doesn't work in this place.
A city with 19 000 inhabitants consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences, roofs made of woolen sheets in shades of gray and rusty colors.
Very few Indonesians, let alone journalists, looked at this place, and practically no strangers.
Before the arrival of the Christians, the locals worshipped a god named Uga Tamee.
And that isn't all.
"We haven't been accustomed to wearing this dress," Bardina says, pointing to her colorful, hand-knitted turban, a dark t-shirt and a bright skirt.
“Previously we only wore leaves.”
Papua is Indonesia’s poorest provincial province—Pliminary people live below the poverty threshold, and child mortality and literacy is one of the worst in Asia.
At the same time, it is an Indonesian country of gold.
Grasberg's largest and highest-grosquilibest goldmine in the world owned by Freeport McMoran from Phoeenix is located only 60 miles from Paniai - a rocky province about the size of New Jersey, which is home to about 153,000 inhabitants.
Freeport mined $3.1 billion worth of gold and corpore in 2015.
In addition, Papua promotes logging at an estimated $78 billion worth.
These Wealth Wealth, however, are a source of Suspiciation for the Papuans and a guarantee of oppression by the mighty Indonesian army.
An investigation carried out by the New York Times in 2005 shows that Freeport paid about $20 million to local military officials and units between 1998 and 2004, including $150,000 per officer.
Papuans calling for self-government endanger this golden goose and are therefore severely repressed.
According to human rights activists, no more than 500 000 Papuans have been killed since 1969 and thousands of others were raped, tormented and imprisoned by the Indonesian army.
The mass murders in the outskirts of Papua during the 70's of the last-century genocide amounted to genocide, according to the Asian Human Rights Commission.
Last year alone, the Indonesian police arrested more than 39,000 peaceful Protestants in the region.
We Will Lose Everything, a report by the 2016 BrisBaden Archdiocese describes the atrocities that occurred last year as out-of-court executions, torturing - according to another report, rape and electrocution - and brutal repression of peaceful demonstrators.
"It's difficult to calculate the number of casualties because incidents occur every week," said Andreas Harsono, an Indonesian Human Rights Watch researcher.
The whole situation was afflicted by the fact that papuan sources attract colonists to Indonesia.
Of the 3.5-ma million inhabitants of the province, 83% espouse Christianity, but demagophite changes with the influx of Muslim immigrants from the Indonesian islands - Java, Borneo, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
Javan canteens sell fried chicken and gado-gado or a mixture of vegetables with peanut sauce.
Local people are fighting for survival.
"Immigrants have started selling chimes and vegetables in traditional markets at lower prices than local Papuans," explains Abeth You, a twenty-four-year-old who was born to Paniai and moved to Jayapura for work.
"The indigenous people of Papua-mama (women) have thus lost their job."
Indonesian President Joko Widodo, nicknamed Jokowi, as part of his 2014 presidential campaign, vowed a solution to inequality and violating rights.
The former carpenter secured his journey to the presidential palace in Jakarta 27 of the 29 districts of Papui, including Paniai.
But there's very little that's changed in Papui, and local people feel betrayed.
"Our hearts have been fractured because in 2014 we voted for Jokpwi with the hope that he would listen to our creels for justice," he said.
The fact is that just two months after Jakowi's inauguration, Paniai suffered a big shock.
On December 7, 2014, a group of 11 children sang christmas songs at the campfire in Enarotali when two Indonesian soldiers on motorcycles emerge from the glitch of the night.
Two of the surprised children told them that they should turn on their lights.
This rebuke and tone in which it was uttered and later came back with four other soldiers, local parish priest Yavedt Tebai said.
Two of the soldiers in a drunken state pursued a group of children and beat them with the butts of their guns, the victims and witnesses of the event said.
One of the Soldiers then Fired a group of children.
One of the children, the 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimo, was beaten so hard that he fell into a coma.
A few hours later, someone undertook an unilateral building of an Electoral Commission, and the next day things escalated.
About 1,000 young Papuan men and women rallied on a football field in front of a local police station and an army headquarters, claiming justice.
Came with ceremonial hunting merchandise and performed a scan of a wait - in a circle that imitates the singing of birds - the Papuan tribe of Mee Pago.
Several Protestants have started throwing stones at buildings.
Rattling with suspensory, the soldiers were given an order over the radio: "If the crowd tries at least three times to resist, you have permission to shoot," TIME said in an official document whose content has never been posted in local media.
Yeremias Kayame (56), head of Enarotali Kego Koto, recognized the immovable danger and urged residents to disperse into their homes.
However, no one neglected his remarks.
"When I've turned around, someone shot me in my left wrist," TIME said on the porch of his russian-painted wooden house.
Kayame does not yet know who the shooter was, but according to him, a bullet belonged to one of the soldiers present.
"Everywhere there were a lot of people and there was a lot of gunfire," he added.
Alfius Youw was hit three times, according to his co-worker who witnessed the shooting.
"I used to run to my body to convince myself that it's really him," Yohanes, who, like many Indonesians, uses only one name, told TIME with a sullen voice.
"I could see he was dead... I kissed him."
The Papuan police inspector, General Yotje Mende, told reporters that his officers merely “provided” a station that was under attack.
"We have to defend ourselves when people are threatening us with their deaths," spokeswoman for the Papuan police, commissioner Pudjo Sulistijyo, said in 2015.
“It is a matter of life and death.”
According to the Human Rights Watch organization, five protesters were killed and many escaped with injuries.
The news of homicides faded by Jaktart media the next day.
Three weeks later Jokowi gave a passionate speech in Jayapure and expressed his understanding for the families of the victims, vowing to deal with the violence in Papuia.
"I demand an immediate solution to this problem so that it can never happen again," he said.
National Security Minister Wiranto announced in October 2016 that he will introduce an out-of-couth mechanism to resolve the violent acts that have taken place in the past.
Almost as soon as there were excuses.
“Most violent acts have happened a long time ago.
Some of them happened in the 90's of the last century and some at the beginning of the 21st century.
“I want to say that we are determined to punish these violent acts, but it won’t be an easy task,” he said.
In an interview on June 5 for the TIME, which took place in Jakarta, Wiranto argued that he did not plan to introduce any measures in Papua.
Instead, he said "every cases will be presented to the court."
Wiranto, whom the United Nations accused of "crimes against humanity" on the basis of more than 1, 1,000 deaths during an independent elections in East Timor - he said that so far 11 human rights violations in Papuia, including an incident in Pania.
Families of the victims of the shooting in Panaria welcomed his claims with disbelief.
"I've been questioned four times in the last three years, but I haven't seen anything until now," Yohanes said.
"I'm tired."
As he says, for a few years later, he still lives in fear.
"I'm afraid," he says.
"I'm afraid I'll be bailed by an army or I'll be shot."
His brother Yacobus has confirmed the perspective of many Paniai residents who are afraid to talk about the incident.
According to him, he was defeated by members of the army after helping to bury four of the casualties.
"After burying my bodies, an army started looking for me," he said.
Gunfire does not stop yet.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police were firing villagers in neighboring Deiaia.
The scuffle, in which one person died and another 17 were injured, occurred between the villagers and the manager of the construction company, refusing to provide assistance in transporting an unconscious man to the hospital.
Ravianus Douw (24), who drowned while fishing in a nearby river, died on the way to the hospital.
Angried villagers protested in front of the company's building.
According to the police, the villagers were throwing stones at the affiliations and the answer was a warning gunfire.
Locals, however, claim that the mobile brigade (Indonesian paramilitary police) started fissosing into a mob, killing one person.
“Damage has come, we were afraid of retribution,” the 29-year-old Dominggu Badia, who lives near the hospital and witnessed the arrival of those injured, told TIME.
“I didn’t leave the house for two days.”
The Deiasia parliament demanded the punishment of the participating police officers and the withdrawal of a mobile brigade from the area.
Paniai has always been a thorn in the side for the Indonesian government.
Lack of sensible development only deepens separating the indigenous people of Mee, Moni, Dani and Damal, who live in the central highlands.
Many of them have joined the Papua Liberation Movement (OPM) - an armch of insurgents that protect the rights of Papuans by attacking and kidnapping Indonesian cops.
Some of the OPM leaders come from Paniai. They belong not to Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
As a result of the attacks in recent years, the army has grunted and arbitrarily deprivated thousands of Paniai residents, all under the pretext of "providing national sovereignty."
Some of them will never be seen again.
Paniai is known among the inhabitants of Papua as a "tragic and forgotten place".
Poverty populates the oil into the fire.
The little rice sold in Enarotali is too expensive for the locals.
Blewha is equally unavailable.
Local residents grow their food: mainly sweet potatoes, fruits and leafy vegetables.
Women work in the fields; each of them cultivates four or five fields with sweet potatoes.
Most of the harvest is intended for the needs of the family, the rest they sell in the local market.
Ten pieces of sweet potatoes cost only 10 000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Since Nabire airport opened more airborne routes and new immigrants have begun to pouring into the country, there has been an increase in economic inefficiencies between native Papuans and Immigrants.
More educated and wealthy immigrants usually occupy so few jobs.
Papuans seldom have the necessary capital and an education so that they can embark on business.
"Young people are moving out of the villages due to the lack of job opportunities and money," John Gobai, chairman of Paniai's tribal council, said.
Thanks to the issuance, the province of Papua is hidden from the world.
Strict rules for foreign journalists also contribute to this.
Earlier at the beginning of this year, two French reporters were expelled - Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp - due to the "short-felt collaborating with the authorities", all this, despite the fact that they received such a rare permit for filming.
Phelem Kine, Deputy Director of Human Rights Watch's Assassin, Jokowi in his election campaign promised to abolish strict rules for journalists and achieve transparency and development, but this has never happened.
"Foreign registries who work in the rebellious easternmost regions of Papua and Western Papua are at great risk: non-recognition of visas and blacklisted," he said in a statement.
Lack of news from foreign journalists' pens means that the international pressure on Indonesia's Papui is largely curtailed.
In March, six pacific peoples - Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuval, the Marshall Isles and the Solomon Islands - the U.N. Human Rights Human Rights Council urged "differentiary and widespread acts of violence" in Papua, including shootings in Pania.
In the past, these countries have also endorsed OPM.
An Indonesian Foreign Ministry spokesman Arrmanatha Nasir shrugged off accusations when he told reporters in Jakarta: "A democratic system is still in place in Indonesia and the media are free, so it is difficult to disguise the evidence of cases of human rights violations."
Local people are crying out for the involvement of foreign governments.
When an official dedication from the Netherlands, led by Human Rights Emissary Kees Van Baar, visited Jayapura on May 4, local people proclamated according to a source who also attended the session but wanted to remain anonymous, the slogan "we want freedom".
Another election is scheduled for 2019 in Indonesia, but papuans say they won't vote for Yokowi.
"Jokowi is a man who has good intentions, but is surrounded by people associated with shooting in Paniai," said Gobau, chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Jokowi to be aware that Paniai residents - in addition to living in constant fear of a plundering armies - are lying in poverty, a lousy education, and a health system.
Gobai argues that Paniai residents, like other Papuans, consider their vote as a “debt,” which Jokowi must repay.
"They don't want money, they demand justice," he added.
Contrary to threats and intimidation in the family of the victims of the Pan-Iarian fisllenges, they continue the last symbolic act of defiance: bury the bodies of casualties on the grounds opposite the police and army station.
Knowing that righteousness will never be done enough, at least he will not allow the responsible persons to forget their crimes.
"A member of our family has been killed," Yacobus said with his head lowered.
“Which can we do?”
