In a statement issued on Wednesday night, Pruitt proposed its version of ozone standards and strengthened the EPA's commitment to collaborating with states using complex processes and meeting new standards within a given time frame.
"During the administration of the previous EPA administration, it often did not meet the deadlines, waiting to be sued by activist groups and the others, agree with the alignment and further set-up of plans," Pruitt said.
“We do not believe in regulation through litigation and we take the deadline seriously.
We are never happy about our statute and the authority that comes from it."
