Thursday was the hottest day of the year.
In the Hodonín region they measured 38.3 °C.
Tuesday's 37.3-degree Celsius temperature record stayed at the top for just two days.
With 38.3 degrees Celsius, which were recorded in Strážnice in the Hodonín region, it was surpassed Thursday, which became the hotliest day so far this year.
This was acknowledged by employees of the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute.
Compared to Tuesday, there were not many temperature records on Thursday.
On Tuesday, records were surpassed or leveled at 35 out of 145 weather stations measuring more than 30 years.
On Thursday, only nine long-term measuring stations recorded record-breaking temperatures.
For example, in Strážnice, Thursday's temperature was the same as the previous record from 2013.
In Lednice in the Břeclav region 37.4 podium surpassed the original maximum by four-tenths of a degree and in Pohořelice in the Brno region the current 37.1 podium surpassed the record by three-tenths.
Both records, as well as at Strážnice, dated 2013.
In the west it was ten degrees less.
High temperatures on Thursday appeared mainly in the south and east of Moravia.
After Strážnice it was the largest heat in Dyjákovice in the Znojmo and Brod nad Dyjí in the Břeclav region, where mercury climbed 37.8 degrees, but did not surpass the record.
On the other hand, in western and north-Western Bohemia, according to ČHMI’s website so far, Thursday’s highest temperatures were only about 22 degrees Celsius.
"Temperatures started to grow there until later in the afternoon," the meteorologists said.
According to ČHM, the current heatwave in the Czech Republic will gradually subside, and on Sunday there could also be significant rains.
However, in most places of Moravia, high temperatures will still remain on Friday and in the south-of-eastern Morava even on Saturday.
China lacks hikers.
Beijing is losing billions due to its visa policy.
For decades, China has been trying to exert its influence without the use of means of power.
Every year it spends ten billions of dollars to support language schools and build universities abroad, pushes its entertainment industry into foreign markets, trying to lure Western tourists.
Beijing is failing to do that.
The local state-of-speaking media talks about the plan to make tourism a driving force for economic transformation and modernization.
However, the number of incomers grew by only 3.8 percent last year and about 80 per cent of tourists came to China from Hong Kong, Macau and Taiwan.
This is caused, among other things, by the fact that despite all its global aspirations, China doesn’t relude foreigners just with open arms, evaluates the situation in the world’s most populous country, an economics professor at the University of Beijing Christopher Balding in an article for Bloomberg.
An example that speaks for itself is Chinese visa policy.
Only 13 countries have non-visa-free travel with China, the citizens of the others have to take out the visa in advance.
At U.S. offices in the U.S., therefore, the mobs are comparable to the celebrations of the Chinese New Year.
A $140 visa, but if you don't want to wait long hours in the queue for it, you have to pay $100 or more for additional services.
Such repetition builds significant economic barriers.
A study of the Journal of Economics and Finance has shown that the need for a visa has been reduced by 70 per cent of visitors.
In 2015, only two million Americans visited mainland China, while Hong Kong, which accounts for less than three per cent of China's gross domestic product, was visited by 1.8-million Americans.
China often exaggerates this problem itself when tourism is used to realize its political goals, such as in disputes with Taiwan and South Korea.
In the spring, when China curtailed transport to South Korea as part of a rift around the South Korean anti-repetition system, South Koreans retaliated and avoided China.
In May, the number of attendees fell 42 per cent year-on-year, while Japan recorded an increase of 85 per cent.
All of this affects macroeconomic stability.
According to an recently-published U.S. central bank, the Fed may have left up to $190 Billion as a result of Chinese foreign trips, while China receives about $35 billion a.m. as part-speaking the currency for incoming tourists.
According to the official figures on the development of the balance of payments last year, China contracted $217 billion in turism last year, which is equivalent to 44 per cent of the entire Chinese surplus in the trade of goods.
A small number of hikers, a country that scrupulously impedes the outflow of capital, is inflicting a very plausible wound.
However, the above-mentioned factors are only one manifestation of more general restraint towards foreigners.
Virtually no one has ever heard of immigrant immigrants.
In 2013, there were less than a million foreigners living across China.
There are more of them in the United States, as well as some individual cities.
Japan, which isn't an interscrucite of immigration, has double.
A number of scientific papers show that Immigrants usually revive economic growth, entrepreneurship and innovate.
However, you can’t practically find them in China.
Resolving these problems will be politically challenging, but the step that is offered is the simplification of the visa apparatus.
Other Asian states, especially India and Vietnam, have recently made great efforts to simplify and reduce visa procedures.
If China wants more foreign hikers and their hard currency, it should be easier for them to get there.
