Burma and Senegal.
Everywhere can Czechs vote?
Registered electors will be able to re-vote in the autumn parliamentary elections on the composition of the Chamber of Deputies from Burma, Senegal or Luxembourg.
In these and several other countries, the Czech Republic has opened its embassies since the last parliamentary elections that took place in 2013.
This arises from an ordinance regulating the supply of ballots abroad.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs has prepared an update, it is currently in the comment procedure.
Four years ago, 10 thousand Czech citizens elected MPs abroad.
"Since the last amendment of the Decree in 2013, there have been some changes that need to be taken into account in these lists," the ministry said.
"In some countries, new embassies have been created, some authorities have been closed, or there has been a change in the level of representation," the document read.
The Decree contains two applausion annexes, the first of which include embibrantic offices to which paper ballots are supplied.
This group of authorities includes representation of the Czech Republic in neighboring countries, i.e. Germany, Poland, Austria and Slovakia.
Another more than a hundred Czech authorities will receive a ballot paper in the print version.
It's their list that changes with the ordinance.
Four years ago, Czechs were able to vote, for example, at the Czech embassy in Libya or Zimbabwe.
Both embassies are closed.
The Czech Republic has also abolished its representation in Bamak, Malasky and Kyoto, Japan.
There will also be no election at the Consultancy General in Donetsk.
The Czech Republic closed it in 2014 due to a war in the east of Ukraine.
They are newly on the list of embassies in Nairobi, Senegalese Dakar's capital and Lusaka of Zambia.
In Asia since the last parliamentary elections, the Czech Republic has expanded its representation to the consulate-general general in Irbil and Zheng-tu, the capital of the Chinese province of Si-chuan.
Voting ballots will also be available for registered electors at Czech embassies in Burma and Cambodia.
The Czechs abroad have had the opportunity to choose deputies at polling stations in embassies or consulate so far four times.
Before each election, the State Electoral Commission will determine the region whose candidates are voting abroad.
The first time it was in 2002, when it was decided among South Moravian candidates.
During the next two elections, the Czechs voted abroad among South Bohemian nominees.
In 2013, the Central Bohemian Region was chosen as well as this year.
The number of Czechs voting abroad is steadily increasing.
Approximately 3,800 people came to the podium at the premiere 15 years ago, around 6,700 in 2006, 8222 four years later.
