Moore's Ford massacre: activists commemorate racist lynching
Ford Mercury from 1948 rode around onshookers Monroe, Georgie and Kodrucal towards the Moore's Ford bridge.
However, before a car with four black-haul passengers and a white driver was able to reach the bridge, a group of white people with guns blocked his path.
The group leader - a middle-aged-mid-haired man in a striped suit - dragged off a cigarette for a long time and glared at the windshield of the car.
Inside, passengers trembled with fear.
"We're going to do that-- and Roger!" the man shouted.
“Bring it to me!”
Silence breaks the roar of a young black man who is pulled out of the passenger's seat.
Luckily, this scene is a mere rebuild of events that took place on July 22.
But when one of the men swaggers a noose over "Roger's" neck, he snuggles the audience with excitement.
Since in 2005, the Moore's Ford has regularly refurbished, reconstructing the fateful night, when two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom and George and Mae Murra Dorsey - were killed by Kukluxklan members.
No one has ever been judged for homicide.
“Confessing that not a single person has been arrested in all these years – even though we still see them in our community; even though we know their identities,” Tyrone Brooks said.
"It's a stain on our history and a burden in our souls."
Brooks is a 71-year-old former congresswoman of the state of Georgia and a lifelong civil rights campaigner.
In his eyes, refurbishment poses a dramatic call for action. Each year, Monroe commemorates that justice hasn't been done enough.
"We are demanding trial prosecution and conclusion of the case, we want peace of mind and reconciliation, but justice above all," Brooks said.
In the meantime, the process of healing and reconciliation cannot begin.
An ensuerial doctor raises on July 27, 1946, one of the bodies of lynching victims at Moore's Ford.
According to the FBI's filaments acquired by NBC News, up to 50 men from Monroe and the surrounding counties were supposed to take part in lynching.
However, no one has been prosecuted so far.
Despite the fact that the suspects remain free for decades, supporters of civil rights allegiance alleging that the two reasons for lynching - politics and sex - have been known to all residents of the city.
In April 1946, based on the Supreme Court's decision, black-skinned residents of the state of Georgia were entitled for the first time to take part in the elections that were scheduled for the summer.
And it wasn't during the wake-up election, according to the FBI, black-haired farmer Roger Malcom should have stabbed white-skinned landowner Barnett Hester - supposedly because of his wife.
It was rumored that Hester had traveled in town with Malcom's wife Dorothy and brought her to a different state.
On July 25, in July 25, 1946, Loya Harrison, a $6 $600 bail for Malcom.
Dorothy and cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey escorted him to the prison.
When they got back home from the prison, just before they enter the Moore's Ford Bridge, a group of suspects blocked their way.
"As it turns out, Harrison has planned the murders," the FBI deduced.
"It is also clear that there has been a secret agreement between state and local law protectors, who not only participated in lynching, but also got rid of the evidence and concealed the identities of witnesses."
Brooks commented more succinctly on the matter.
“It was a massacre because of suffrage,” he said.
“Mideshes had a clear subtext for all local black-skinned locals: ‘this is how you end up if you sign up and vote.’”
Cassandra Greene has been organizing an annual refurbe reconstruction at the Moore's Ford Bridge for nearly a decade.
Her performance is imaginary, eye-caution and acquainting. It demonstrates deep-rooted racism, which many would like to consider ephescinary in modern America.
It's not an easy way to watch this rebuild, and it's even more difficult to avert your eyes, but Greene says it makes sense.
"No one wants to talk about lynching, because in doing so we have to deal with unpleasant feelings that are hidden in all of us," Greene said.
“But there can’n’t be a relationship between us if you don’t hear what I had to go through.”
The main rehearsals of this year's refurbishment began in June, and on the very very first day approximately 30 actors - a dozen Caucasians, the remnant blacks - rushed into a room where Greene was atrocilled with a very carefully planned performance.
Wade Marbaugh takes the position of an auxiliary director.
Kukluxklan leader played for many years, but according to his words the role is exhausting.
"I've had a bad feeling because it contradicts who I am," Marbaugh said.
“I think it is very important to commemorate this part of history on a regular basis, because we never want to go back to such a time.”
Actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascon, who play Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester, remanufacturing their role.
The show starts with a scuffle - Malcom is arguing with Hester and accuses him of falling asleep with his wife.
"Getting off my land n---e!"
Rascon doesn't.
There's a scuffle.
At that moment everyone (at least every black actor) erupts in laughter.
Greene is laughing the most.
"What's the matter?" Rascon asks.
He's confiscated.
I was too rude?
"No," Greene says happily.
“I just don’t think the white people were ‘n--r’ in that tone at the time.”
"Exactly," one black actor blushed.
“You have to put more emphasis on R.”
Energie in the room is suddenly better; hostility will replace a sense of rapprochement.
They all want the show to be Authentic, if only to be made behind the four victims of the massacre.
Actors are recounting the scene.
Rascona puts an emphasis on the correct utterance of the word on N.
Nobody's laughing anymore.
Introducing ends forcibly and quickly.
Screaming, gunshots... and there's silence.
A woman dressed in black stands over the bodies of the arcade and sings the grieving song of Billie Holiday "Strange Fruit".
With every word, there is sadness in the room.
In the background of all this, about a dozen onlookers wiping away sweat and tears from their eyes.
There's practically the same number of blacks and whites in the audience.
A couple came all the way from Ithaca, New York, many onshookers are from nearby.
Each of them puts up the local wet clumsy for almost eight o'clock, and Brooks in the crowd leads them to the tomb of Malcolm and Dorsey.
Up to this point, murders may have been viewed with hindsight, but now, standing in the same place where lynching took place, the atmosphere is haunted.
With the most recent words of the song, Greene will be challenged by a mob onlookers to inspect the bodies.
The children knelt towards the heads of the victims, watching fake blood and immovable bodies and whispering something in their ear.
"They aren't dead," the little boy said.
A few minutes goes by, and Greene will thank the audience. It's over.
The actors are cleaning the rope and wiping away their tears.
Bradshaw hugs Marbaugh, a man in a suit starred by the Kukluxklan leader.
Nick Rascon hugs his two friends-a-a-a-a-a-a-a-actor, cringing in their arms; in the end, emotions affect him.
"It's all right," one woman whispers.
"We know you hate the role.
“You had a reason for that.”
Later on the same day, the audience sits together with the actors for dinner.
At the same table, a young white-browered couple are talking about a performance with a black couple they got to know today.
Denise Duplinski had scarcely found the words to express her feeling about today's performance.
“It’s difficult to listen to those nauseous words and look at those disguissive deliberations... that are done by people who look like you and do it because they look like you,” she said.
"It makes me realize that lynching is still a topic-to-responsible topic," she added, "it's just called police scuffles."
Tyrone Brooks sits in the courtroom on the other side of the room. He enjoys dinner and talks about civil rights.
According to his own words, he has already started planning the show for another year.
“Renovation of these events is anspiring because it describes the fear that we had to experience – but at the same time it’s a moment to think,” he said.
"If we don't learn from it, who knows... it could happen again.
People who don’t know and don’t study the history of their country are doomed to repetition.”
