Pearson lays lays lays off an additional $3,3000 and lowers its dividend to revitalising business
Britain's Pearson education group on Friday said it would lay off an additional 3,000 employees and cut an interim dividend by 72 per cent in response to another restructuring program in order to change the business model.
Another wave of redevelopment, which, according to executive director John Fallon, will take place at the end of 2018 and earlier in 2019, is followed by a previous decision taken by 4,000 employees as part of a previous restructuring.
Pearson announced a plan to reduce costs by an additional £300 million ($3.94 million) in May as part of a third cost-reducing program to resurrection declining commerce as a result of the rapid development of digital education.
The company, which last month subpoenaed the sale of its stake in Perguin Random House to Bertelsmann, said the outlook for this year remained unchanged after announcing a one-pericent increase in base sales in the first half of the year to £2.05 billion.
