Responsibility in response to lawsuit reconsider its decision to delay smoking rules
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency has revoked its decision to deduit the Obama administration's law, which mandated states to reduce emissions, just a day after 15 states sued the agency.
The EPA announced its decision to cedent "Ozone Standards 2015" on Wednesday, saying it was presenting an agency's commitment to co-operating with states.
"We believe in dialogue with our state-owned partners and we will listen to their views," EPA director Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
Pruitt in June announced an attempt by the EPA to delay ozone standards - which measures actual air pollution in specific areas of the country and, based on measurements, determine the limits that must be met by October 2018.
Fifteen from most democratic states, along with Washington, D.C., filed a lawsuit on Tuesday on the grounds that the EPA's conduct is illegal.
U.S. President Donald Trump's administration seeks to avoid many environmental regulations proposed by former President Barack Obama - Trump's words in an attempt to kick-start economic growth.
