Investigators believe the death of a young couple was an accident caused by an icy road leading around the river.
Investigations, however, are ongoing and further details will be published after its completion.
Resunes Test Results to Determination of the Aggree of Assembliution Appliances in Sweden
Relevant asylum seek tests in Sweden who have been subjected to a new method judging their age show that more than 80 per cent of people were surveyed over the age of 18, but not everyone believes their accuracy.
The Swedish Forensic Medical Agency (RÃ¤ttsmedicinalveckerket) began conducting new tests earlier this year.
The aim of the method is a more accurate assessment of applicants in the context of the evaluation of asylum applications. The measure was approached after the Swedish Migration Agency (Migrationsveket) was criticized for failing to determine the correct age of certain refugees who lied about their age.
At this date, the Swedish Migration Agency has forwarded 6,6880 cases to investigate and the Forensic medical agency has now published the results of a total of 24,481 tests carried out from mid-March to July 31, 2017.
80 per cent (2002) was assessed as over the age of 18, while in a further 25 cases the Forensic medical agency decided the interviewer was "maybe over 18 years of age".
In a further 423 cases, it was decided that the interviewee was “perhaps under the age of 18”.
The tests are only approached if the age of the interviewer is questionable.
According to Johan GÃ¶ransson of the Forensic Medical Agency, the results collude with the previous month.
"Compared to last month, the stats are on the rut and the numbers are very similar," he told The Local.
A higher number of contenders whose age has estimated at the age of 18 does not necessarily imply that asylum seekers lie about their age.
In May, the agency published the results of 581 tests and stated that in 442 cases it had been found that “a person in question is 18 years or older”.
The Svenska Dagbladet newspaper investigated 581 published cases and found that in 243 cases the respondents said he would be 18 years this year.
Criticism has also been criticized by the medical method of determining age, which includes an X-ray of Wisdom and MRI skeleton scans of the knee joints, which are subsequently judged by dentists and X-rayologists.
One skeptics is Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute, who, among other things, deals with the assessment of the biological maturity of people to determine the age when the person underwent puberty.
“I had a reason to think about this issue and I don’t think these types of age-asserment measurements are correct.
Judging a biological age is, in a certain sense, very uncertain.
“If we can’t determine an age with precision, we shouldn’t pretend the opposite.”
Agency's GÃ¶ransson advocated the forensicipating: "In general, it can be said that the risk of misassessing a child as an adult is small.
The highest risk poses the fact that the interviewed person is just below the age of 18, which in this case corresponds to 10 per cent of those surveyed.”
Trump jeopardized on the phone of the President of Mexico.
U.S. President-elect Donald Trump, in a January phone call from Mexican President Enrique Peño, has threatened not to meet him if he said publicly that his country will not pay to build a wall between the U.S.-Mexico.
Trump said in an interview that funding will somehow be arranged, although he has acknowledged that it will have to be ensured from other sources.
Similarly, Trump spoke openly to Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull.
This is according to transcripts of interviews published by The Washington Post on Thursday.
Trump made the construction of a wall on the southern border for Mexican money one of the main vows in his election campaign last year.
Immediately in the first interview with the Mexican president shortly after he took office, Trump rebuked Peňa Nieta undiplomatically for stabbing himself critical of his plan to build the wall.
He recounted the southern neighbor as a growing political problem, pushing the Mexican president not to publicly say that his government will never pay for the wall.
"You can't say this to the press," Trump said repeatedly in an interview on January 27, threatening to cut contacts with the Mexican president if he continues to issue defiant statements.
The financing will come up somehow and it will turn out well in the end.
"But if you're going to say Mexico isn't going to pay for the wall, then gentlemen don't want to associate with you anymore, because that's unacceptable to me," Trump said.
At the same time, he described the wall as the least important thing we are talking about, but politically it is perhaps the most important.
An executive order for the construction of the wall was issued shortly after taking power.
Mexico denies the 3200-kilometre-distance construction and financing of the 3,200-kilometre-long relations between the two countries have also escalated.
At the end of March, Trump said the United States was going to pay for the project for the time being.
Trump finally reunited for the first time with his Mexican counterpart at the G20 summit in Hamburg in July.
The Washington Post also reported on Trump's telephone conversation with Australian Prime Minister Turnbull of January 28.
The head of the White House described the conversation as ridiculing.
At such a conclusion he was prompted by an exchange of views on an agreement, according to which Australia will grant asylum to Central American refugees in exchange for that the United States will accept persons from internment establishments in the Pacific island states.
At first, Trump regarded the deal as a sissy, but he later accepted it.
"Certainly you can say that this isn't the deal you've created, but you're going to have to cling to it," Turnbull said.
