West Virginia Gouter Announces Change of Political Party
Democrat governor of West Virginia, Jim Justice, announced on Thursday evening at a rally of President Trump that he was crossing into another party.
“Whether you like it or not, the democrats have turned their backs on me,” Justice said.
“Today, after much prayer and deliberation, I am announcing to you... today, as a resident of West Virginia, I am telling you that I can no longer support you as a democratic governor.”
A crowd of onshookers erupted into joy.
"I'm going to join the Republicans tomorrow," Justice added.
"As a coach, I can tell you that it's time to jump into another game."
Trump has previously pledged an important announcement to people at a rally in West Virginia.
Trump won in West Virginia with 42 points and Justice did not support Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton.
Judiciary, who according to Forbes magazine is the only billionaire across the country, was elected in 2016.
Forbes adds that Justice has inherited great possessions from his father and is the owner of several coal-fired mines.
The value of his assets is estimated at $1.59 billion.
The Republican National Committee, meanwhile, on Thursday evening issued a statement stating: "The overthrow of the Governor of the Judiciary is another blow to the democratic party that puts obstacles in our way, instead of elevating our country."
“For a democratic party that possesses only 15 governors and controls the least legislature authorities in history, the announcement by the Judicial governor is the last straw for an already miserable party without a leader that seeks in vain to find positive and unifying news,” RNC chairwoman Ronna McDaniel said in a statement.
Sebastian Coe Praises 'Hearty' Russian Apology for Doping Scandal
Former outstanding athlete Sebastian Coe commended Russia on Thursday for an sincere apology for a doping scandal, but reiterated that it has so far not been the right time to return Russia to the sporting world.
The head of Russian athletics Dmitry Shylakhtin said on the occasion of the IAAF congress - which took place on the eve of the Athletics World Cup - that the expulsion of his country from the world of sports was right and that he is determined to fight doping.
Apologized "to all athletes, who in this way depridiated for gold and silver medals."
Contributed to his comments, IAAF members voted to maintain a ban that came into force in November 2015 after the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA report) showed that Russia had promoted doping its athletes.
"I think we've witnessed a very sincere response and presentation today," IAAF President Sebastian Coe told reporters.
"All the members of the council and the Congress were pleasantly surprised that Russia had admitted its flaw and is working with all their strength to correct and restore the proper functioning of the entire federation."
Coe said he was also "pleased" that Russia had adopted criteria for its reintroduction.
"I think today was very successful and we have progressed to the next level, but the congress supports the crisis staff's recommendation to maintain the punishment imposed on Russia," Coe added.
“In the coming months, we have to do our utmost to stabilise the situation.
Our aim has always been to separate honest athletes from dishonest ones,” he said.
19 Russian athletes will compete under a Neutral flag at the Athletics Championship, which begins on Friday in London.
IAAF Crisis Director Rune Andersen said on Monday that Russia still needs to meet a number of criteria before being allowed back.
Tests for the presence of drugs are still unsatisfactory and excluded coaches continue to carry out their activities, he told reporters in London after the presentation of the IAAF board.
