How the financial rules of football will affect Neymar's move to Paris
Neymar’s record-breaking transfer from Paris-Saint Germain, France, in France, will not disrupt Neymar’s record-breaking transfer from Spain.
Any penalty for Paris Saint-Germain will be carried out by EUFA, which oversees European football teams and has the power to ban teams from participating in the prestigious Champions League.
The Financial Fair Play (FFP Rules), which introduced UEFA in 2011 stipulate that PSG will have to prove that Neymar's transfer has not been financed without major losses.
Barcelona only paid €222 million to redeem Neymar out of his contract and Neymar will demand a salary of several tens of millions of dollars a year.
This is followed by several financial ties of Neymar's transfer to France.
Since the PSG team is funded by a wealthy Qatari-familiar who has become rich in energy sales, it can afford to bring Neymar into an already stellar football family.
The clubs can spend huge amounts on the purchase of players, but on the other hand they have to prove the statutory sources of income, which allows them to cover the overshadow costs of business activities related to football.
FFP introduced Michel Platini after being retired to UEFA director's office in 2007.
The former French player believed that the clubs, which would become indebted behind new reinforcements, are ultimately cheating and risk bankrupt if banks or landlords refused to secure them.
In 2011, FFP rerouted its attention from club debts to monitor their losses.
This change meant the business model used by Roman Abramovich with Chelsea in 2003 - to buy a club with potential and spent exorbitant sums on players to build a global brand - suddenly it wasn't as advantageous.
As part of the current three-pericent probationary period, which ends in 2018, clubs can report a loss of up to €30 million.
And despite Platini having to abandon his post in 2015, his successor Aleksander Čeferin is determined to advocate a ban on over-threshold.
“FFP rules are still in place and we are determined to strengthen them in the future,” Andrea Traverso, who overselegates FFP’s compliance with FFP, told the Associated Press last week.
Although PSG has paid an amount for redemption of Neymar in a one-time payment, it may, for the purposes of accounting, decompose an amount over the entire duration of the contract.
Neymar has signed a five-year contract, so that the transfer can be budgeted at EUR 44.4 million every year over a period of five years.
The FFP's first-lane sanctuations was hit by PSG in 2014, along with Manchester City owned by Abu Dhabi.
PSG received a fine of EUR 60 million ($82 million) - which was later reduced to €20 million - and an order to re-reute the Champions League team to 21 players in the 2014-2015 season, instead of 25 gamblers.
UEFA pays attention to teams that may try to circumcise financial regulators by exhaling their income through companies associated with club owners.
Thus, even if the Qatari company sponsors PSG, as soon as financing exceeds the value of the standard market rate, UEFA has the right to deny the bill of the amount.
A similar measure was taken by UEFA in 2014 after deciding that the amount of funds provided by the PSG Qatar Tourism Authority was too high.
Neymar's portion of Neymar's income in PSG could be reimbursed, according to some reports, from an organizational commission of the 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar.
PSG and World Cup 2022 leaders in Qatar, however, have denied that Neymat should become an embaster of the FIFA World Cup and that such a manoeuvre would easily be detected by UEFY officials.
Previous sanctions imposed on PSG mean UEFA will closely monitor any attempt to circumcise the rules.
According to Tuesday's initiated, UEFA did not begin an investigation into PSG's transfer of Neymar to PSG.
The source of the report wished to remain anonymised because he is not entitled to comment on the situation with PSG, The Associated Press said.
The last published financial statements show that PSG closed 2015-2016 with a net profit of EUR 10 million and a turnover of EUR 542.4 million.
Enggregations for the current financial year have not yet been disclosed.
PsG expects Neymar's arrival will be a huge opportunity for sponsors, merchandising and jersey sales.
From the signing of the contract, the club promises to increase turnover by up to 30 per cent.
Representatives of the Spanish league, apparently concerned about the departure of one of the biggest stars, sought to thwart Neymar's departure, arguing that PSG will break the FFP rules.
“It’s a clear case for ‘financial dopupination’ of the club and the state,” la Liga Javier Teba president told Spanish newspaper AS, citing the ownership of the PSG club with a Qatar syndicate.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger, whose club owns an U.S. sports magnate, shares La Liga's equal stance on the club's financing.
"It's a consequence of ownership that has fundamentally changed the football world in the last 15 years," Wenger said on Thursday.
“As soon as the owner of the club becomes, anything is possible.
Compliance with the rules of financial fair play is very difficult, because the state can have different interests in the participation of such players in its club."
