A businessman is supposed to sit down for an extreme tax evasion for ten years.
The Constitutional Court has dismissed the complaint of businesswoman Jiri Toman, who is supposed to serve ten years in prison for cutting taxes to a significant extent.
Quantified damage amounts to 645 million crowns.
Toman argued in the complaint that the judiciary had violated his fundamental rights, the factual findings of the courts in Zlin and Olomouc are said to be incompetent.
However, constitutional judges described the complaint as unwarranted, ČTK learned from the court's database.
There is no further appeals court.
"It cannot, as a fourth instance, consider factual or legal misjudments allegedly committed by general courts if they do not constitute a violation of constitutionally protected rights and freedoms," the resolution said.
Toman was judged fugitive.
The verdict has been final since November 2015, when it was reaffirmed by the Olomouc High Court.
At that time, Toman was in dispensing custody in Argentina, where, according to the resolution, it was located even at the time of decision-arrangement.
The penalty imposed is at the upper limit of the legal rate, which, according to the verdict, corresponds to the damage incurred.
The high court described the case as an extreme tax evasion, which only has a macroeconomic impact on the state budget.
From 2005 to 2008, the man served as the Chairman of the Board of Directors of World Servis.
Škoda CZK 645 million originated only in the period from January to July 2008, according to an expert, the total ejection exalted a billion.
For procedural reasons, it was no longer possible to sue Toman for the full amount.
According to the indictment, World Servis did not include all invoices in its tax returns.
Instead of at least tens of millions of crowns of VAT and excise duty in the order of at least tens of millions of crowns, she paid taxes in only hundreds of thousands.
World Servis and some of the companies with whom the company has traded are related to the entrepreneur Radomír Vybíral, whom the judiciary has been sentenced to 13 years of imprisonment for tamubling VAT on fuel transactions.
His complaint was also dismissed by the Constitutional Court some time ago.
"Summer to Survive" and "Comrade Detective" series play on nostalgic streak
This iteration of the story is nostalgic for characters from the glorious 80's; as well for the filthy 90's (when geniuses David Wain and Michael Showalter launched a comedy sketch "The State") on the MTV; and, of course, for the modern era, 2001, before stars like Elizabeth Banks, Amy Poehler and Paul Rudd flickered into larger projects.
As a cultural artifact it is a slight parodie and at the same time the embodiment of our modern Facebook era of perpetually memories.
From the point of view of franchise, however, it seems that someone is running out of breath.
"First Day of Camp" worked surprisingly well - not only because of the absurd humour (an an example could be a story about the emergence of a talkative can of vegetables, which was voiced by H. Jon Benjamin).
The authors diverged from the main witty point - middle-aged actors who play teens horny children - and couldn't make fun of previous events.
A sequel "Ten Years Later", in in which adults play adults don't put so much on an emotional level and satire is sometimes sporadic, including a parodie on 90's thrillers as "Hand on the cradle".
At one o'clock, the character finds in a pile a haraburdy Apple Powerbook, roller skates and "a lot of other things from 1991."
Many of the main story lines are repeated.
A bizarre Coop (pan Showalter), now a writer, still hasn't crossed his love for Katie (Marguerite Moreau).
Victor (Ken Marino) is still a virgin.
Not to mention an insane plot that includes Ronald Reagan (also Mr Showalter), incentivized by George H. W. Bush (very well-occupied Michael Ian Black).
As always, the best part of the franchise are many side stories.
It’s especially amusing to watch an ever-young-year-old-year-old young Rudd alias the character of Matt Dillon from the film Youth, whose once-respectable character Andy is now an old paprik.
A franchise could be kick-started by moments of random frenzy, such as "Seven Up" recurrence until it achieves what I'm assuming it's going to be a logical denunciation: "Wet Hot American Summer: Twenty Years Later," when, in the last scene, the whole group embarks on the premiership "Survival."
"Comrade Detective" on Amazon in the meantime appeals for a different kind of nostalgic: the revivalism of the Cold War "Such-normal Americans," "Atomic Blonde: Without Regret" and even the "GLOW", which clashed icons of the Eastern and Western Wrestling Scene clash.
A comedy directed by Rhys Thomas by the creators of Brian Gatewood and Alessandra Tanak is supposed to act as a televised production: a drama about two Bucharest police officers from Romania in the 80's of the last century, fighting for truth and atheism along the manner of Marxism-Lenvinism.
The main roles - tenacious Gregor and the idealistic Ioseph - starring Florin Piersic Jr. and Cornelia Street, which they dubbed the English language Channing Tatum (executor of the series) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt.
It's an amazing idea.
I'm afraid it's not even much more.
With some jokes you laugh (such as police officers watching chess on TV - parodies for American football).
Many well-familiar academies have lent the series their voice - among them Jenny Slate, Nick Offerman, Mahershala Ali and Chloë Sevigna.
"Comrade Detective" focuses too much on the clumsy propagandistic drama - which also includes a killer with Reagan's cassock - and that's what it looks like.
Six long episodes are too scramblous and there aren't enough comedic moments.
With a shorter footage, the propagandistic satire in "Comrade Detective" could work, such as in a scene where Gregor and Iosef repeatedly visit the U.S. Embassies, in the hall of which two fat people devour hamburgers stacked in a big pile.
A clear message for an imaginary communist academies is the fact that Western culture can turn everything into food and orgy.
Deliberately Romanian creators made no mistake: we do so with our own culture, as well as with theirs.
Russian athletes did not see the lifting of a ban on IAAF starts.
The IAAF international athletics federation at a congress in London has confirmed an extension of an international ban on the start-up of Russian competitors due to state-sponsored doping program.
Voting in the venue of the world championship, which will begin on Friday, turned out very unequivocal as expected.
166 delegates voted in favour of an extension of the ban, only 21 votes.
The main reason for the rejection is mainly the fact that Russia fails to meet all the requirements for the establishment of an anti-doping system, as stated before the Congress by the head of IAAF Special Investigation Group Rune Andersen.
His group recommended that the ban continue.
Some older doping cases remain unresolved, a number of suspected athletes haven't yet been questioned, and there are still covencrucible coaches in the country.
The Russian anti-doping agency RUSADA, which does not meet World Anti-Doping Agency’s standards.
RUSADA, however, can already carry out tests under the supervision of foreign experts and British anti-doping agencies.
"Even though there has been progress, there is still a lot of work to be done to do," IAAF Chairman Sebastian Coe said.
The Russian athletics federation was segregated in November 2015, Russian athletes lost, among other things, last year's Olympic Games in Rio de Janeiro.
In spite of the ban, the upcoming World Cup will be attended by 19 Russians who have previously demonstrated that they have been tested for a long time outside the Russian anti-doping system.
However, they won't be able to compete in national colors at the championship and they won't play the Russian anthem.
The Bank of England will hold an interest rate at 0.25 per cent
As expected, the Bank of England is expected to announce that the interest rate will remain unaltered, following the lackluctuary of economic growth and a weakening of inflation that has stimited speculation about sharp growth.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain "behind" in the context of maintaining an interest rate - despite squeezing family budgets.
Politicians of the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) of the Bank of England have voted six to two to maintain an interest rate of 0.25%. The results of the poll for an interest rate hike supported the lacklustre economic growth supported by a slight increase.
In its Annual Inflanation Report, the bank predicted growth at 1.7% in 2017 and 1,6% in 2018, warning that the reduction of family budgets will continue. The bank expects inflation to rise to 3% in the fall.
At the same time, the bank has hinted that over the next few years there should be a rise as a result of inflation caused by Brexit, and it has also stated that movability rents should rise more in the financial markets than expected.
The members of the committee also voted to withdraw part of the mammoth package, whose task was to support the economy and which was put into practice a year ago in response to Brexit.
As a result of the foregoing from next-followment, the Temporary Financing Plan should come into effect from next-indecision, offering cheap subsidies to banks, although the Bank of England said it will now offer £15 billion less compared to £115 billion.
Just prior to the decision, the bank stated: "MPC predicts that gross domestic product (GDP) will stagnate in the short term, along with pressure on real household income and the burden of consumption."
On the topic of rates, the bank said it would be necessary to “pully tighten the belts of monetary policy” to sooby inflation, “at a higher rate” than the markets.
Markets are currently predicting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and a further increase in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that any increase would be "graduous" and "limited."
The bank downgraded the growth forecast for this year's and next year compared to 1.9% and 1.7% predicted in May.
Expenditure growth of 1.8 per cent remained unaltered in 2018.
The pound fell against the dollar and the euro.
The pound fell 0.5% to $1.31 and by 0.4% to 1.11 euros.
The decision to maintain the current situation came after a disappointing shrouding hopes for further growth - GDP halted at 0.3 per cent in the second quarter, compared to 0.2% of the previous three months.
The increase is very likely to remain at 0.3% in the third quarter, albeit should increase slightly towards the end of the year, according to the bank's statement.
Inflation's latest report is a shrullen consolation for households affected by rising inflation and miserable salaries. The bank has said the situation will get even worse before the surge.
The report further states that the monetary policy "cannot prevent" income cuts in the coming years, but wages are expected to "significantly" rise at the end of the three-year forecast.
Economies should also rise due to the great interest in British goods due to weak pounds, computing a lower percentage of consumer spending.
This decision came a year after rates were reduced to 0.025 per cent last August in response to a smoky EU referendum vote, which caused the pound to fall.
The bank estimates that a complete unification of prices and the economy will last for at least four years.
Before the bank's decision on the latest rates, many demanded an increase in rates, along with rising inflation, which exerted increasing pressure on households. Three political officials demanded an increase to 0.5% in June.
Inflation last month fell back to 2.6% from June's 2.9 percent, even though the bank said it had anticipated such a change and inflation should rise again over the course of the coming month.
The bank stated that exceeding relative to 2 per cent the target was “completely” as a result of a weak pound.
Andrew Montlake, Coreco Mortgage Brokers, said: "Given the recent fall in swap rates, it looks like the competition in the mortgage market will only rise as creditors begin to compete for contracts with a look at a good outlook at the end of the year.
The market looks especially good for those ending in existing contracts and re-mortgage - there are no fees-free options on offer, as well as the low rates they gain in popularity."
The NHS presupposes a controversial HIV-drug 10,000
As of September, patients will gain access to “transformative” treatment to HIV prevention based on the Supreme Court’s decision to finance the NHS drug.
The English National Health Service (NSH) has announced that pre-exposure prophylaxis (PreP), which is taken prior to sex and reduces the risk of infection in high-havioable patients by up to 86 per cent, will be offered through health service to approximately 10,000 patients as part-of-year probation.
NSH agreed to finance the probationary period after an appellate court's decision last year, stating that the NHS disposed of PrEP funds despite stating that the drug should be funded by local authorities.
Announcing a decision announced on Thursday was accepted by the NHS of England from the mouth of CEO Simon Stevens as a 'significant intervention', while the National Aids Trust (NAT) conceded the decision as a "decissory moment".
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to start accepting patients. Other clinics will join in October, and by April 2018 there will be a complete implementation across England.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is an HIV drug administered before sex to reduce the risk of HIV.
Excerpts have shown that PrEP significantly reduces the risk of HIV transmission among at-at-thrifty patients.
PrEP application before sex means that there is a sufficient number of specific drugs in the patient's body to prevent infection.
These drugs can be taken every day or at least two hours before sex.
Patients tested include homosexuals or bi-sexual men, transgender people, and people with HIV-positive partners ungraied.
The clinics shall designate eligible participants who will consent to participating in testing, including men, women, transgender people, and individuals with a partner whose HIV status is not yet supervised by appropriate treatment.
People living and registered with the GP in the United Kingdom can also sign up at their local sexual health clinic and apply for potential participation in testing.
The program will cost taxpayers £10 million, and NHS England has signed a treaty to distribute medicines this week after winning an international public contract.
The examination period is designed to collect clinical evidence and to assess the full potential of PrEP, its targeting, the use and implementation on a large scale.
Simon Stevens added: “This important intervention should complement and fully exploit an ever-expansing and increasingly successful anti-horrhousing effort.
“It’s another milestone in a more than thirty-year effort to stop one of the greatest challenges for human health.”
Deborah Gold, Managing Director of the NAT (National Aids Trust), said: “We are witnessing a decisive moment in the fight against HIV.
If PrEP is administered correctly and at risk to patients, it will offer the possibility of a transformation of the English HIV outbreak.
NSH from September will offer patients who are at a high risk of HIV infection a new powerful tool, which is individually controlled and is not subject to the need to persuade a partner, which will lead to an improvement in the quality of many lifetimes.
We accept this statement with pleasure.'
Ian Green, Managing Director of Terrence Higgins Trust said: 'Priority number one must be the rapid deployment of tests across the country; we must ensure its availability to every patient.
We now have a pre-producing drug at our disposal and we are on the right track to protect more than 10 000 people who are at the risk of contracting HIV.'
Shadow Health Minister Sharon Hodgson said: “The start of the PrEP trial period is a welcome and long-awaited change after months of deferral and detention by the government.
Evidence suggests how transformative this drug can be as part of our approach to preventing HIV and terrestrial transmission of this life-threatening infection.
Subsequent tests will lead us one step closer to a full understanding of PrEP benefits.
Now it is important that the tests begin as soon as possible throughout the country to protect people who are exposed to HIV and together end the spread of HIV in society.'
Life on a high foot: an mezzanine and a smart design have turned this little apartment on Earls Court into a spacious home
Joanne Leigh, a former banknote with a passion for real estate, exchanged a big house in Knightsbridge for an apartment on Earls Cour in 2014.
Situated on the first-floor of the Queen Anne smart terrace - and is an illustrative example of the modern design, which takes advantage of the most accessible space.
"Before I lived with my ex-husband in a much larger house in Knightsbridge," Joanne said.
“I’ve been looking for something at a more sensible price.
I stumbled across this apartment in Earls Court and it had the same ceiling height.
I immediately fell in love with him and on the same day I nodded at him. "
Since then, an apartment of 1,000-square-metre has undergone complete refurbishment.
A one-room apartment with a complicated layout - in order to get into the bedroom, you had to go through the kitchen - Joanne wanted to add storage spaces and a mezzanine to take advantage of a high ceiling.
“I longed for a high ceiling, large windows and lots of light.
Such were my demands," she said.
For this purpose, Erfan Azadi of Duck & Shed's design and design studio in Notting Hill and used a nearly four-metre-long ceiling to create a home that wasn't just a project, but also a place to live.
Ceiling did not reach such a height that two loopholes would fit above each other - the challenge for the architects was to create a mezzanine on which you could go upright.
Erfan had to engage his creative thinking.
"Superstructure is quite complicated," he explained.
“We had to organize both spaces in such a way that we could walk upright.
“It wasn’t just a matter of building a simple platform, but at the same time to bend the steel structure so that at the right height above and under the ceiling.”
This arrangement has allowed Erfan to construct two full-fledged-loving rooms on top of each other - while at the same time adding to the drama.
On the mezzanine he overlooks the beautiful living room of the old copper window frames in Retrouvius design.
Mirror furniture in a gold color, silver lines and a retro chandelier Serge Mouille creates a unique style.
The use of mirrors plays an important role in drama, space and illumination.
A short hallway is equipped with mirrors on both sides and looks longer.
The mirror bar in the kitchen looks like a window for the next room.
At the entrance door there are storage compartments equipped with mirrors to store cutleries and jars.
The advantage of the living room, which gives the whole apartment the impression of a significantly larger space, is the opportunity to separate it from the kitchen if it is not used.
Complainted kitchens are nothing new.
However, what is unique about this solution is a folding door.
"We've had to solve the technical side," Erfan talks about reinforced veneered doors.
"The door is provided with a counter-basin so that it rolls away very easily."
Thanks to the clever use of the space and storage space, Joanne received an additional 16 square meters, a second room and a better overall layout of the apartment.
"When it finds out, that's what you're selling is a residential area - or the valuation of your property," Erfan said.
“The real estate agent basically calculates the price on the basis of a living area when entering an apartment.”
In this case it was possible to elevage the living area while maintaining high-quality materials that give the space a luxurious appearance.
“Material is very important because you’re responsuring it and creating an emotional bond with your home,” Erfan said.
"You won't fall in love with electricity in the walls.
“Yes, they’re important, but they don’t gain your hearts.”
As it turned out, high-quality design and an increase in the living space can turn an ordinary apartment into an exceptional work.
And just like Joanne, in the end, you can choose to stay.
The U.S
The cultural and political abyss between the blue and red states is deepening.
California has imposed a ban on the travel of its citizens to the state of Tennessee and seven other states.
The state of Tennessee retaliated and declared a retaliatory ban on traveling its citizens to California.
Of course, it is not a prohibition in the full sense of the word, U.S. citizens can travel freely in all fifty states.
It's that California has forbirants of spending state money on the journey of its citizens to eight other states such as Alabama, South Dakota, Kansas, Kentucky, Mississippi, North Carolina, Tennessee and Texas.
Who's going to stop this madness?
Calipidoria is strongly for the LGBT, and it sees any expression of dissuscine with the LGBT lifestyle as discrimination.
However, Tennessee has envisaged a Conciliation Act, according to which the psychotherapist, who has a moral problem with the therapy of patients from the LGBT community, does not have an obligation to provide them with it, but has a duty to provide these patients with another therapist who does not have such a problem.
In turn, Texas and other states have passed laws under which religious charitable charitable organizations that received children from mothers after birth for adoption do not have the obligation to provide same-genuminence to same-sex couples.
Due to these laws, California's conscience objection has banned the financing of its citizens' travels to eight EU states by its means.
In practice, this means that Californian professors must not have travel to scientific symposia, for example, reimbursed from their graduations from their Universities, because they are co-financed by the state of California.
Likewise, school sports teams cannot travel to tournaments or competitions, for example, to Tennessee with the financial support of their schools.
They either have to obtain federal grants for travel, but it is absurd for the federal government to finance citizens' trips from state A to the state B, or private sponsorship.
The state of Tennessee has run out of patience and his parliament has passed a law with a resolution that he also displeases California's high-level tax policy, high state spending, high deficits and tolerance of illegal immigrants, he doesn't impose a travel embargo on California.
He puts them in re retaliation for the California travel embargo on Tennessee.
And the same will happen to each state of the union, which would order a similar travel ban on another state for any reason.
At the same time, he urged the parliaments of all states to stop this insanity.
Facts, now a comment.
Situation is toxic.
First of all, all of this is constitutional, nothing is unconstitutional.
States have the right to enthusigate laws, as well as to decide what their money will go for and what they are not allowed for.
Secondly, what's wrong with eclipsing your conscience?
When the butcher has a religious problem to sell pork, why can't he only offer beef, lamb, poultry and fish?
Why, under sanctuary, compel him to sell porpoise, when there is enough butchers offering pork?
There is a baker in the Colorado state facing an anti-discrimination lawsuit and a hefty fine because he refused to bake a cake for a gay wedding.
It also refuses to bake cakes for Halloween or liquor-containing.
Why punish him and not leave it alone and not to re-arrculate the appropriate cakes from hundreds of bakers of others?
Thirdly, the beauty of the federation system is that the constitutional rights of all citizens of the federation are guaranteed, but individual states can adjust the internal, domestic policy in competences that are not federal, according to the preferences of their inhabitants.
Then there are variance, diversity, diversity.
California can be California, and Texas can be Texas.
However, California wants California to be California and Texas, and seven other states to be a California.
Four-thirty, the members of the LGBT community once rightly sought tolerance for their lifestyle.
Today, the many members of this community follow the rule of living and allow themselves to be guided by all sensible people, but some activists strive not for tolerance for themselves and others, but for extreme intotolerance towards others.
According to them, no one can ever again express moral, religious or philosophical disagreement with an LGBT lifestyle, and if they do, it should be criminalized.
Mentality of these activists is totalitarian.
They are the modern enemies of freedom.
Firm, I don't know the situation in U.S. history, when the states were doing so on purpose because of the political issues, except for a single case.
Even at the time of the struggle for the civil rights of negrowers, where the North and the west were on the side of integration and the south on the segregation side, the states did not impose sanctions on each other.
The only case was the 50's of the 19th century, the political caucus was slavery and the war of the North against the South was brewing.
Of course, there will be no such thing now, but the situation is still toxic.
The cultural and political dispersal between the states of the blue democratic and the Red Republican states are deepening, and the U.S enthurry is mentally unbelieving.
Unauthorized agitation.
Critique of Putin received a greasy fine.
Russian opposition politician Alexei Navalny has been fined 300,000 rubles for violating campaign rules ahead of the presidential election, which will take place next year.
The court fined him for inviting people to participate in unauthorised demonstrations.
Navalny has orchestrated two major anti-government street protests over the past two months, announcing that he wants to seek the post of head of state in March next year.
Kremlin chief Vladimir Putin has not yet announced his candidacy, but it is widely expected to do so and win again.
Navalny said the police came to his house this morning and took him to the court.
He fined him for inviting people to demonstrate via videos posted on the YouTube internet portal.
The fine is three-hundred-and-a-thousand.
"Later we're going to take them out of the judge's salaries," Kremlin critic joked on Twitter.
The Russian Electoral Commission recently concluded that Navalny could not take part in the presidential election because he was previously convicted of embezzling embezzlement.
Navalny, who is believed to be an anti-corruption fighter, sees the verdict politically motivated.
Fines of 300 thousand rubles, i.e. 25 thousand rubles by the court on Thursday was also imposed by two members of Navalny pre-election team, Leonidu Volkov and Nikolai Ljaskin.
Navalny entered Russian politics as an anti-corruption blogger and gradually became a dominant figure on the fragmented opposition scene.
The authorities try to defend Navalny in every possible way in the campaign.
In February, he was sentenced to a five-year suspended sentence because he errobbed Kirovles.
The verdict was upheld by an appellate court in May.
The verdict is thus final, which, according to Russian law, prevents Navalny from running.
Breess battling an empire worth more than $500 million
The two brothers now, for more than four decades after the founding of the empire by their Italian family, tug-of-threshold one of the most expensive Victorian real estate worth more than $500 million.
Former Carlton Football Club and developer Colin De Lutis resides on Toorak estate, just three rooms from his younger brother, Paul, who has driven a dispute over joint assets and business fortunes to high court.
Paul wanted to leave the family business as early as 2014, but a sharp controversy is constantly denounced before the supreme court.
Along with their mother Anna, the brothers own a total of more than half a Billion-billion-dollar real estate. The first store was bought by the family in 1973 on Smith Street in Collingwood.
In this place, the family founded Westco Jeans, which has grown into a chain with 160 brick-and-mortar stores.
In 1999, the family released $85 million for the entire company.
With the money they bought additional real estate, and the De Lutis family's portfolio now includes offices, building plots in Brunswick and Sorrento, Gold Coast and Mt Buller apartments, and shopping centers on the outskirts.
Paul owns approximately one-three of the company's assets, while his father Colin, who, according to Paul's "significant driving force and dominant personality" of De Lutis, owns less than two-three assets.
Litigations say Colin owns $316-million worth of real estate while Paul manages real estate for $176 million.
For Paul, it has just become a difficult distribution of assets and funds.
"Caolin's suggestion sounded markedly in his favor in my eyes," Paul tweeted in an affidavit in 2015.
Last Friday, after several unsuccessful congrution attempts, the first meeting of the Supreme Court took place.
Brethrowers are now also competing to see who will control an existing business - from which, according to his statements, Colin cut off.
Colin contradicts the fact that this is not true and accuses Paul of mishandling some properties.
Colin said in a previous affiliated statement that he believed in a successful resolution of the conflict.
"My Italian origin dictates to me that my family is the most important," he wrote.
Hundreds of affidain statements, e-mails and messages from companies including Deloitte, Pitcher Partners and Charter Keck Cramer overwhelm lawyers including Allan Myers, QC and defense advocates of both parties.
Mrs De Lutis and her deceased husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who ran milk bars and delicacies from the 50's of the 20th century and in the 70's bought a shop in Brunswick.
With the money raised by seizing the assets, they bought the property in Collingwood, where Colin opened his first jeans family business.
Colin said that when he founded Westco "in the last year of college, Paul was still a small, about ten-year-old boy."
Anna De Lutis said she and Luigi, who died in 2012, had hoped that the transfer of the property to sons would take a good time.
"Luigi often used to say that he never wanted his sons to end up in court," she wrote.
The other two brothers, Mark and Luke, are no longer part-of-company De Lutis family group. Colin stated that Mark was compensated by $10 million and Luke with $26 million.
Speaking by Paul, both Luke and Mark "were not satisfied with the terms of their financial compensation."
Colin has been holding back since, despite the fact that he is a former member of the AFL club's board of directors, as Eddie McGuire, acquaintance television personality and best friend Colin, said in his 2015 autobiography.
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul de Lutis and their lawyers on Friday.
Responded only Colin through a text message stating that, in view of the fact that the dispute is being dealt with before the court "I have nothing to add".
“It is very sad that my brother Paul has decided to deal with the matter on trial,” he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin said in a statement in court that he was trying to adapt to his brother's demands.
"Our father would be very dissatisfied if he had seen our current situation," he said, adding that he "still hopes for a successful solution."
On Friday, there was no hope in disregard.
"Our controversy will be resolved by a court hearing anytime soon," Colin wrote.
