The Code of Behavior determines the presence of an Italian police officer aboard ships, such as Iuventa to monitor activities.
Only three of the nine non-governmental non-non-profit organizations involved in rescue surgeries so far have previously agreed to comply with the code: Save The Children, the Maltese MOAS and the Spanish Pro-Activa Open Arms.
The latter organization said on Thursday that the new rules were unnecessary, but acceptable as they do not represent any "essential change or obstacles" in terms of rescue surgeries.
The adoption of a new code of conduct was also rejected, for example, by Doctors Without Borders (MSF), which is a Nobel Prize winner.
Minniti, meanwhile, has said that a suprence in immigrant immigrants in recent weeks looting in Italy as part-of-rescue rescue operations suggests that efforts to bolster patrols off the coast of Libya and collaborating with local mayors bear fruit.
