How did an activist find the answer to the question: ‘Whose Streets?’
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrell shuddered all over her body.
The day after she arrived in New York, the St. Louis native sat down on a bed in an Airbnb room and went through Facebook posts on her phone.
She came across the contribution of a high-grade school pupil.
“Police have just killed an 18-year-old young man whose body is still lying on the street.”
Surprised that no one else had written this post, so she closed Facebook and opened Twitter.
User @TheePharoah constantly retweeted her wall posts.
A rapper from St. Louis posted on Twitter his posts about the death of Michael Brown, an unarourated 18-year-old young man who had been shot by a white-skinned policewoman in Ferguson, Missouri.
Published a photograph of Brown's body lying limlessly on the street, from where no one took it even after four o'clock.
"It's crazy," Ferrell thought.
But death wasn't anything new for her and her community.
She dumped the phone to come back to him in the evening when she watched tweets about the people who were colliding on Canfield Drive.
One of the men claimed that the victim's father and was holding a sign in her hand with "Ferguson police killed my unarranged son"?
She was watching live news - one of the policemen was standing in front of a group of protesters, holding a leash in his hand and at the other end of the barking dog.
In another video, another policewoman, she said, held a gun in his hand, with which he was aiming at the mob and roaring: "I'm going to sausage all of you... animals."
"That's not normal," Ferrell said.
"This isn't the kind of murder we've become accustomed to, which we can call normal.
I felt goosebumps all over my body.
“I was angry and I cried.”
Immediately she booked a return flight and travelled home the next day.
After landing at 23:00, she picked up her six-year-old daughter from her father and decided to go to Ferguson in the morning.
The story of Ferrell and Ferguson over the next-three years describes a new documentary "Whose Streets?" that will be released in theaters on August 11.
It's a story about survival and protests, love and resentment, power and resilience narrated from the point of view of people living in the community who take to the streets and call for answers.
On the way to Ferguson, Ferrell introduced a dialogue with her daughter - dressed in a floral jersey and the same floral headdress - about a theme they had previously discussed many times - "about the experience with blacks."
"Remouflage how I've been teaching you how black people had to fight for what they believed in?" he recalled.
"I'm going to Ferguson right now because the police have killed an 18-year-old and that's not right."
"It wasn't possible that I didn't take her with me," Ferrell said.
"Today's events are the culmination... that happened in the past.
She must've known and see it with her own eyes and grow up.
It needed to develop not only in activism and organizing, but at the same time it had to know the difference between the different layers of experience with black people, and what we have to do in order to get better.
Even though I’m capable of providing conditions in which she doesn’t experience racism on his own, they don’t differ in any way in any way from another black-female-woman.
“I feel that if I protected her from this experience, I would just hurt her.”
Arriving in Ferguson, South Florissant Road's first stop was opposite the police department, which became the base of the activists, awaiting the words police officer Darren Wilson about his eventual involvement in the incident.
That's where Ferrell and her Daughter got acquainted with a local businessman who's been preparing food for others.
Together, they prepared food for the sachets and helped distribute it to the Protestants.
In Ferrello's eyes, a new type of activism slowly began.
"My activism was turned upside down after August 9," Ferrello said, adding that while attending the University of Missouri-St. Louis, she was the president of Minority Student Nurses Assn., striving for the introduction of health care and food in black communities.
“I didn’t have an experience with organizing.
It was natural.”
A few weeks later Ferrell made acquainted herself during an in-of-night demonstration with a later co-director, Sabaah Folayan.
Folayan said he and her cameraman Lucas Alvarado-Farrar wanted "only to document" ongoing events.
Ferrello, however, thought: "Are you working on a documentary or are you trying to find a story that could be exploited?"
This kind of skepticism permeated the community through so many people, often white people who wanted to profit from pain and decisiveness.
As Folayan explained in an unlaunch interview: "We have heard that the voices of people who are born and grow up in Ferguson are very often neglected and we wanted to change that."
Folayan has already collaborated with co-regiter Damon Daston Davis, who is an acclaimed local artist who deals with the theme of deprivations in death row.
Ferrello also involved his involvement in the project, along with six other locals, who were followed by the "Whose Streets team?" in the years following Brown's assassination.
"No one speaks for us in this document, but it's our testimonial," Davis said.
"That's what was important to me - how these people were going to be represented - because I'm going to be represented in the same way."
“While it wasn’t aimed to focus Ferrello’s experiences—in an attempt to show that the movement “isn’t a savior-to-header, but of community rapprochement,” Folayan said.
"Whose Streets?" documentary thus devises the very personal aspect of Ferrello's life, for example, when she fell in love with her wife.
“I feel naked,” Ferrello said with a saushuahuahuahua yoke at the first time she saw the final version of the document.
"But I've come to understand that in order to be able to do this work and humanise black people, I had to open myself."
In addition, Ferrello's story emphasises the ecclesiastic black woman's ecclesiasci-to-centric aspect of the Black Lives Matter movement.
Two of the three founders of the movement - who, in response to the killing of teenager Trayvon Martin in 2012, on social media campaign under #BlackLivesMatter - call each other weirdo.
"Black ecclesiascy women in leading positions ultimately stand behind the preservation of the movement," Ferrell said, "because we are constantly fighting on multiple fronts.
Black eccentric women must withdrift the weight of these events.”
Folayan agrees with this statement and adds that “The movement is in fact supported by black women and a lot of eccentric black women.”
However, he insists that “this is not a mere affirmative affair” and Ferrell is not the main heroine of the documentary because of her views.
“It was she the driving force and the source of energy,” she said.
“That she found herself on the front lines isn't a coincidence, because life at these crossroads in the skin of a black strange woman is dangerous.
You can even feel the rush of energy in front of the screen.”
In the meantime, back in Ferguson, the cameras of the television stations have vacated their positions, but heavy racial bonds between the community and the police persist.
Ferrello is serving two-and-a-halp-year probation while blocking the highway during demonstrations.
The social-political vibe in an area she calls home described "Flatter."
Nevertheless, she continues her tribute, to which she has pledged since she took to the streets of Ferguson three years ago.
“It is our duty to fight for our freedom.
It is our duty to win.
We have to love and support each other.
“The only thing we can lose are our chains.”
