A Becoming Resemblance: an artist creates portraits using Chelsea Manning DNA
In the center of one of the rooms of New York's Fridman Gallery there are several cheeks of different skin tones - white, black and squid - hung from the ceiling on the wires. Each of them seemed to fall out of the previous eye.
The artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg created all 30 portraits from the swabs of the oral cavity and her hair sent by Chelsea Manning.
Manning sent her samples from Fort Leavenworth Prison, where the ex-analyricist is serving a 35-Year-dear sentence for unauthorised disclosure of classified WikiLeaks.
The new exhibition, which was launched on August 2, is titled A Becoming Resemblance.
It is the result of a two-year correspondence between Dewey-Hagborg, which combines her experience with technology, computer science and art, and Manning - a transsexual and groundbreaking dissident whose punishment was diminished by Obama three days prior to his departure as President of the United States.
An Algorithmic analysis of Manning's DNA and utilizing 30 Portraits to illustrate what a person with this genotype would look would have made Dewey-Hagborg a rabell, if not an Intel Intellectuary's subservience - as well as the many ways in which DNA can be interpreted - and an inseparable determinism of these interpretations, as well as an identity.
Manning, who was born Bradley Edward Manning since her young age, has talked about being considered a woman. She was sentenced in 2013 on the basis of twenty indictments, including violating six spy laws, computer scavenging and the theft.
All her photographs were deprived by prison staff. Only one black-and-and-immediate photograph escaped to the public, at which Manning, visibly concerned, sits in the driver's seat in a platinum blonde wig, with which she is now associated.
In 2015, there was still a positive reaction to the 2012 Stranger Visions Dewey-Hagborg project.
As part of this project, Dewey-Hagborg used various forensic objects - such as cigarette butts and chewing gum - to create a picture of the possible forms of these people.
And it was at this time she received an e-mail from Paper Magazine.
"They were preparing an interview with Chelsea Manning in prison and they wanted to fill the article with a portrait," Dewey-Hagborg explained on the occasion of opening a new exhibition.
However, visits and photography were not allowed, so they contacted Chelsea asking if she would be interested in a DNA portrait.
An artist and her imprisoned muse became vacant within two years through correspondence.
They even created a comic book called Suppressed Images, Illustrated Shoili Kanung, who presented a vision of what would have Happen if Chelsea were pardoned and could take part in the exhibition in person.
A few days after the comic, President Obama actually asked for Manning's dismissal, a welcome change after several unbearable years spent in a Kansas military prison.
Indeed, it is interesting that the exhibition takes place just as Donald Trump is waging a war with people seeking to publish government information and trans-gender soldiers, while trying to discover new ways to insumerge with his tweets in areas he should support.
Responding to a query about this special timing, Dewey-Hagborg stated that "everything is happening for some reason."
Under the statement of the artist on one of the gallery's walls, Manning calls with a huge signature (the letter "i" in the surname is tapped by the heart) after the "self-restricted faction, which is based on gender, race, sexuality and culture".
On the opposite side there are the sequences of her mitochondral DNA - a centifer of the letters C, G and A recorded with a pencil.
By incorporating 200 letters (or nucleotides, according to a sophisticated scientific lecoder of an artist), Dewey-Hagborg wanted to illustrate how surprisingly similar, at least in biological terms, we are.
"I hope that people get away from it, that it doesn't depend on our genome who we are and that genetic data is subjective and can be interpreted in their own way," Dewey-Hagborg said.
“DNA tells a lot of different stories, these are 30 of them.”
The last specimen of the exhibition, located in a separate room, is a single page from the above-mentioned graphic amendment.
It shows Manning, which emerges like King Kong from United States Disiplinary Barracks with a mega-per-fight in his hand.
“As soon as he prevents you from speaking, they will win,” it said.
"Never be silenced."
"It is a direct transcript from one of the letters," the Philadelphia artist said.
"When I think about it, my goosebump jumps."
Manning has not yet personally visited the exhibition - which has been predicted in a comic - but when this happens, she will be greeted in the center of the room with her masks, her own genomic effigies, lined as a mob of Protestants.
This was also the purpose of the exhibition, including the nearly 160 cm tall Maning statue, on which her genetic haplogroups are scored with a pencil.
"In the past they were placed on the walls, but this time I wanted them to radiate the feeling of a crowd," Dewey-Hagborg said.
“I wanted to give the impression of a mass movement that takes the form of Chelsea and liberates it.”
