"In the near future we will complete the WADA plan" - Deputy Prime Minister Mutko
Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko said it wouldn't be long before the residual World Anti-doping Agency (WADA) criteria and the re-establishment of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA).
"Currently, part of the criteria of the new plan has already been finalized and RUSADA has already gained the right to planning and testing in cooperation with the British Anti-Doping Agency (UKAD).
In view of this, the WADA plan has been up-to-date," Mutko said on Thursday.
The vice-presidant commented on the second part of the WADA plan aimed at complying with the code - which describes the criteria for re-resumpival RUSADA - which was recently published on the organization's website.
According to Mutka, the Code does not contain anything that Russia should be alarmed.
"Everything has been announced and everything will also be implemented.
The results will be available to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) and WADA in the near future," he added.
However, one of these criteria is calling for a public announcement of the results of WADA investigation investigated by Richard McLaren about the alleged sponsorship of Russian athlete doppelgage by the government.
Mutko said: "We are conducting an investigation because our anti-doping system clearly failed.
All measures have been taken.
However, there were no state programs in our country, so we cannot confess to something that has never existed."
Mutka's remarks were endorsed by Vitaly Smirnov, director of the Russian Independent Public Anti-Doping Commission.
Speaking in an interview with the Russian RSport Smirnov, he acknowledged the problems of Russian anti-doping officials, he added: "We have stated on numerous occasions that the report contains congruent stances and regulations.
No one plans to accept the message completely unconclublished," Smirnov concluded.
WADA suspected RUSAD of taking out doping controls within Russia in November 2015 immediately before the doping scandal.
Nevertheless, the organization allowed planning and unification of testing under the supervision of international experts and the British Anti-Doping Agency (UKAD) in June this year.
After a visit to the Moscow headquarters of the organization in July, WADA CEO Olivier Niggli said RUSADA was "on the right track" to resuper force.
The police have charged three people and a juvenile.
The police have accused three men of founding Wednesday's fire of a rare wooden church in Třinec-Guty in Frýdek-Místice.
One accused is a juvenile, all three are of Czech nationality.
They face up to 15 years in prison for the crime of general endangerment.
The Moravian-Silesian-Silesian-Silesian police director Tomáš Kužel told reporters today.
According to him, it was an intentional offense.
"It's a very fresh matter and the police are currently working on the motive for the whole matter," Kužel said.
He stated that the police will discuss the possible take-up of the accused into custody.
Further information, according to the director, cannot be disclosed.
"I can't say any more so far, because there's really a criminal police and an Investigation staff are actually working on the matter," Kužel said.
The wooden Church of the Body of God in Gutech came from the 16-th century.
It was a valuable monument with many original equipment and paintings from the first half of the 16-th century.
According to preliminary estimates, substantible damages will go into tens of millions of crowns, but the historical loss is incalculable.
Soon after the blaze, the restoration of the church began.
Most of the costs should be covered by an insurance policy, additional costs are ready to be shared by the Ministry of Culture and the city, a public fundraiser is also being Prepared and it is counting on a contributor to the owner of the monument, i.e. local parish.
A similar wooden church of St. Kateřina from the 16-th century in Ostrava-Hrabová was also devastated a fire fifteen years ago.
This was caused by a glitch on the wiring at that time.
An accurate copy was created in its place.
The state envisages citizens in the defense of the state.
It will support their training.
With the financial support for associations usuable in the defense of the state, with voluntary shooting training or with a team of training trainers, the substantive intent of the law on the readiness of citizens to defend the state.
The Ministry of Defence, which has been tasked with its processing by the government, promises to increase the physical proficiency of the Czechs as well as their abilities, which could then be used by the state in crisis situations.
This is according to a proposal prepared by the ministry.
If the law were to be enacted in the future, the state would expend hundreds of millions of crowns in support of defense.
The main objective of the proposal, according to its authors, is to create an effective system that would help increase the readiness of the inhabitants of the Czech Republic to the defense of the state, in addition to the practice of military duty and on the basis of voluntary nature.
For example, the state would, for example, financially or material support associations of radio amateurs, shooters or cynologists and would make it possible to increase the capabilities of their members.
In return, he would obtain data on people who are willing, if necessary, to come the country to help in times of the crisis, and on their capabilities or material equipment.
Accompanied instructors could also be available to assist the associations and other interested parties in training or education.
It is also counted towards organizing discussions with veterrane veterans or people who have behaved heroically.
One possible option presupposes that a new office would be created, which is in charge of the administration and coordination of the project.
The authors of the suggestion emphasize that the participation of people should be voluntary and the state would use their abilities only after declaring a state of emergency.
"The whole concept must respect the gradual blunting of previously obvious boundaries between the different types of threats," the inquisition said, citing the dangers of so-called hybrid threats.
Ministerial clerks have noted that it is therefore necessary to focus on supporting the widest possible range of abilities, not just military ones.
"The basic objective of the intent is to establish a very broad base that will allow every citizen to respond skillfully to situations threatening the sovereignty of the state or the lives, the health and property of persons, to protect himself, or to contribute to the protection of others, and improper behavior does not impede the fulfillment of tasks by the armed forces of the Czech Republic."
Adoption of the law would bring considerable financial demands.
According to the Ministry, their amount will depend on the chosen option.
For example, the Office envisages financial support for projects to prepare citizens for the defense of the state up to 100 million crowns.
If an administrative and coordinating centre were established, its operation could cost around 60 million crowns a year.
Additional money would go to salaries or to build an information system.
The proposal also envisages that the scope of regional military headquarters would have to be expanded, which together would employ an additional 42 soldiers.
Representatives of the ministry have previously stated that the law should also help reconsider the disagreement of some Czech politicians with the introduction of a directive of the European Union concerning the possession of weapons.
Its adoption would allow shooters who will be part of the system to retain some of the weapons required by the directive.
Startups that will help your company don’t become another Uber
Amy Errett wanted to measure the level of employee satisfaction of her e-commerce startup, but she did not find an adequate survey.
Responses were often inaccurate, useless, or even misleading.
And despite promising the an anonymity of the survey, some employees expressed their disbelief in the whole process.
“I was looking for consistency and objectivity,” Errett, the e-commerce company’s e-company Madison Reed-based in San Francisco, which employs 75 people.
So she asked for help from the outside.
New human resource start-ups are constantly appearing on the market to help companies with employee satisfaction surveys.
By concluding and licensing software with a specific intent to measure employee responsibilities, startups allow start-ups to take quick votes, target specific teams and demographics, also offer total employee anonymity and a hot complaint line, and, in some cases, also allow whisgisers to circumnavigate their superiors and to contact directors directly.
"There are tools on the market like Strava and Fitbit to monitor your state of health, but where is Fitbit for your company?" said Jim Barnett, co-founder and chief executive of Reedwood City-bearing-based Glint.
Errett said Glint gained greater awareness in three years about what her employees are thinking and what they feel.
In addition to the rapid surveys and voting of specific teams, Glint offers the company's temperature map and a trivial overview of groups with the highest number of complaints, as well as an overview of low-evaluation managers - which allows the user to work on the remedy.
Companies are beginning to realize that it is necessary to pay attention to the culture of the work environment - in case they become another Uber - which is tossed in scandals after a former employee posted a post on his blog describing the corporate environment and harassment of employees who dare to speak openly.
For start-ups such as Glint, the demand for corporate culture polls represents a lucrative business opportunity.
According to Let's Talk Payments, which deals with technical and financial progress, the value of the global control, regulation and compliance industry may exceed $118.8 billion by 2020.
Convercent start-up from Denver, Colorado, which assists companies from preventing and detecting inappropriate behavior, has seen a slight surge in interest and activity at the start-of-the-teaching year, just before Uber scandals.
Nearly 600 Convercent clients include Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber has also recently embraced under its flank.
Similar to Glint, Convercent allows Convercent to send customizable “flash” surveys, collect confidential information in real-time and display temperature maps of problem areas.
It also offers an anonymous text hotline that allows employees to report inappropriate behavior.
If the director-general is implicated into the problem, complaints are sent directly to the Board of Directors.
"The Judicial Door of Public Opinion is empowered by regulators," said Patrick Quinlan, the founder and chief executive of the startup Convercent.
If a company is convicted of mishandling its employees or unethical behavior - and if regulators do not intervene - it can face costly consequences as a result of boycotting customers, depletion of employees or legal proceedings, Quinlan added.
The Ruby Tuesday restaurant network, which employs more than 25 000 people across 500 locations, has used Convercent for more than a year to raise employee awareness of corporate policies and treatments, ensuring an easy-governing way.
Earlier, if an employee wanted to report any problem, he had to find a phone number or an e-mail to the company's management, officially file a complaint and hope for a successful solution.
Frequencies was a deterrent and annoying experience, James Vitrano, Deputy Governor of Ruby Tuesday, according to which there was previously no reliable way of tracking employee complaints.
Now that the company uses Convercent, it is ensured to quickly detect and resolvultom problems that have previously never reached managers, seated in the offices, far away from the actual resolution of complaints - for example discrimination, harassments, or unfair treatment.
"It's approaching a holistic, overall view of employees' experience," Vitrano, who manages the Ruby Tuesday group overseeing risk-manage-management, said.
"At the same time, we protect shareholders from legal litigation on the part of employees."
The companies ceased to take away the ethics, values, and participation of employees lightly in 2002, after accounting firm Arthur Andersen collapsed after Enron's fall on the misuse of employees' ethnic rights, Quinlan said.
But it wasn’t until “social media shone at the whole thing,” society began to realize that in the online world, they couldn’t simply hide their skeletons in a closet.
"Ever before people started using technology to monitor the ethics, they relied on faith," Quinlan added.
Glint and Convercent offer their software in the form of a service, for which companies pay regular fees.
It is a business model and an opportunity approved by courageous capital investors, supported by both start-ups.
As part of its February campaign, Convercent raised $10 million as part of its February campaign from companies like Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, eventually earning $47 million.
Glint raised $10 million from Bessemer Venture Partners in November and gained $60 million throughout the campaign.
These investments are scarcely surprising due to the interrevalence of societies, culture and venture capital.
Increasing research clearly confirms that today's employees are expecting more from their workplace than ever before.
And in competing markets such as Silicon Valley, high salaries and interesting projects aren't the main advantages.
Entrepreneurs want to perceiv that they are being accepted and appreciated and that they are devoting their time to a company that strives for a positive mission.
"If people are happy in their work, they perceive that their voice is being answered, and that their work is rewarding, striving for the success of society," said Nina McQueen, vice-presercent of the global benefits and employment benefits of LinkedIn, which uses both Convercent and Glint.
Expensors make a profit if companies thrive and companies are thriving if its employees are satisfied.
And when an analytical tool appears on the market to ensure the engagement and commitment of employees to the company, it is not surprising that there will be a lot of investors who will support this effort.
According to occupational culture experts, it is important to have data on employee satisfaction.
But the data itself is pointless if the company does not take the necessary steps.
The fact is that as soon as the company asks for feedback on the part of its employees, there may be big changes.
And if the change doesn’t happen quickly—at the worst time at all – it can cause the disappointment and disinterception of employees.
“As soon as you ask 4,000 opinions you have to be prepared for 4,000 conversations,” said Russell Raath, director of consultancy at Kotter International, who has witnessed many companies that relied too heavily on the data.
"Since as soon as you don't get things done, the employees start to ask the question: 'Did they hear me at all?
Do they care about me?
And if you don't, so why should it interest me? '
Madison Reed, which now regularly conducts an employee reconnaissance every month, can solve the problem within one day thanks to Glint, Errett said.
After gathering feedback from employees who were frustrated with slow decision-making and quality of communication, Errett was able to reorganise several teams within the company and begin communication training to successfully solve the problem.
Ruby Tuesday gains a very important insight into its employees and hopes that in the long run it will achieve high-intensity employment in the industry, which is known for frequent lay-offs.
“If you don’t strive to create a transparent working environment, employees won’t trust you,” Vitrano added.
"And as soon as you start losing employees, you'll lose customers.
And with the outflow of customers, the whole company goes bankrupt.”
Thursday became the hottest day.
With 38.3 degrees recorded in Strážnice in the Hodonín region, the hottest day of the year in the Czech Republic.
It surpassed Tuesday, which was originally the hottest day of the year with 37.3 degrees.
This was reported by employees of the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute.
Compared to Tuesday on Thursday, there were not many temperature records.
Vedra hit mainly Moravia, it was mostly cloudy in the Czech Republic.
On Tuesday, records were broken or equalized for 35 out of 1,45 meteorological stations measuring more than 30 years.
On Thursday, only nine long-term measuring stations set a record.
For example, in Strážnice, today's temperature was the same as the previous record from 2013.
In Lednice in the Břeclav region today 37.4 degrees surpassed the original maximum by four-tenths of a degree and in Pohořelice in the Brno region the current 37.1 degree exceeded the record by three-tenths.
Both records, as well as at Strážnice, came from 2013.
High temperatures today appeared mainly in the south and east of Moravia.
After Strážnice it was the largest heat in Dyjákovice in the Znojmo and Brod nad Dyjí in the Břeclav region, where mercury climbed 37.8 degrees, but did not surpass the record.
On the other hand, in Western and north-Western Agencies, according to the CHMI website, today's highest temperatures were only around 22 degrees Celsius today.
"Temperatures there started to grow only later in the afternoon," the meteorologists said.
According to the CHMI, the current heatwave in the Czech Republic will gradually subside, and on Sunday there could also be significant rains.
However, in most places of Morava there will remain high temperatures on Friday and in the south-eastern Morava even on Saturday.
