Creation of a graphical novel: the story of A Castle in England
Scotney Castle recently starred in Squeeze’s music music music music video, and Margaret Thatcher found her refuge during the 70’s and 80’s of the last century.
Scotney, however, from his cosy site by the Bewl River in Kent, UK, witnessed 700 years of turbulent history that inspired the curly-haired Jamie Rhodes from the county - a well-known predilection in historical documents - to create a graphic novel.
"The sight of the writing of a person who is 300 years after death fascinates me," Rhodes says with a serious face.
“When I hold a letter in my hands, I feel a personal connection.”
The graphic novel A Castle in England documents the life of families who dwelt in Scotney Castle from the Middle Ages until the Edwardian period. Each story is illustrated by a different British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg.
But it’s not Rhodes’s first archive-inspired book: it is also the author of Dead Men's Teeth and Other Stories (2014), a collection inspired by documents of the British Library - a project that was very emotional for him.
"I was intrigued by the imprint of an infancy on a letter written by a double agent in the 70's, who worked for the Jakobits," he said.
"That's the imprint of his thumb!
And letters always smear like smoke, because at that time you had to light a candle to read.
When I sniff a letter now, 300 years later, it's like I've come back in time.
How I Love the Archives!”
For someone so fascindated by old papers, support by the National Trust and Arts Council England and the ability to dwell for almost four months in the castle is almost like a dream.
Rhodes spent his time exploring the Victorian "new" castle, walking through archives and studying many treasures and curiosities, such as a hyena on the wall and bottles that had been dumped for centuries in a ditch.
He strolled on the property and researched the ruins of an old castle, he spoke to gamekeepers, gardeners, and occasionally also artists who worked on illustrations for his book.
At night "he drank whiskey and wrote".
Scotney's confidant history isn't quite an unfamiliar, as the National Trust didn't get access to documents until 2006 after the death of the last heiress, Elizabeth Hussey.
When Rhodes reached the site ten years later, employees were just beginning to penetrate the archives, which Rhodes described in words, such as “Aristocrats for many centuries threw their hands with the words: ‘Pretend it in the attic.’
Diaries, letters from the time of the British Empire, maps depicting property: "You may think the dispute over land is boring," Rhodes said, "but I've encountered one case in which two families fought over birch.
“Keep the people!”
Some stories in the book use the castle as a springboard and dive deeper into English history, including a story titled The Labourer, which follows a man who leaves the newly built castle to join the English Uprising in 1381.
"I've found a plausible footage at 1380, which was called Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex," Rhodes said.
"The English uprising actually began in this area and it occurred to me that a rebellion was a good starting point.
In the end, they have something to do with each other.”
Other stories are tiltedly tied to the history of the castle - for example The Priest, who tells the story of a Jesuite priest who hid Darrell's family for seven years during an English Reformation.
Or The Smuggler about an 18th-century smuggler named Arthur Darrell, who supposedly staged his own burial when he filled the coffin with stones (which was not discovered until a few years after the coffin was uncovered).
Rhodes' favorite family is Darrell: "I was intrigued by the male generation - the men at that time were always indebted and spent money on things they couldn't afford.
A variety of Darrell families have dealt with this by marrying wealthy or old mistresses - I'm imagining they were handbreadful gentlemen who liked to mop their heiress heads."
Rhodes searches for other adventures with an end to the castle.
He recently retired from a two-week walk in the Finnish wilderness, where he drew inspiration for his next book on Elämänmäki, a former sanatorium from the beginning of the 20th-centicurse that was absorbed by nature.
It's a special place for Scotney.
"I was sad when I left," he said.
“I said to myself, ‘Now that I’m going to go back to real life.’”
