The government's quo in Flight continues.
In January of this year, in an article With the Care of a Proceeded Farmer, I also wrote, among other words, about the camp in Lety u Orlík.
The essence of the problem stays the same, so I would like to commemorate the crucial passages of the article.
We've been talking about a Lety camp for many years.
For readers who do not know exactly what it is about, it will probably be the best if they look, for example, on the official pages of the cultural monument Remembrance Place Lety.
There they will learn at the beginning of the historical overview that the camp stood two kilometers east of the municipality of Lety, that it served as a labor camp from 8 August 1940, from January 1, 1942 it was a collection camp, and from August 2, 1942 it became a gypsy camp.
The next text describes the course of life in the camp, including non-human conditions, until March 1943, when the first Aerial Prisoners were enclosed in an Auschwitz transport.
On May 4, 1943 370 people were transported to the extinguishing camp.
There were a total of 420.
A very mournful history of immeasurable hardships of human beings, inflicted by human beings and degenerating ideologies.
There is no doubt that it is necessary to constantly commemorate these events.
There is also no doubt that the remembrance should take place at a memorial in the place of the suffering of these people.
The problem lies elsewhere.
During socialism, the camp was somehow somewhat forgotten, and in the 70's of the 20th century there was a dormitory in the vicinity and on a part of the former camp.
The history of the camp was accidentally discovered in 1992 by Paul Polansky, a memorial was unveiled in 1995.
In the mid- 90's of the 20th century, the government promised to buy and demolite the pig farm, but it has not yet happened.
In 1998 the monument was pronounced a cultural monument and since 2009 its administration falls under the Lidice Memorial.
In July 2013, the UN Committee on Human Rights urged the Czech Republic to close.
That's so briefly the facts about the aftermath of the camp's post-war fate.
It wasn’t until last year’s autumn that the government approved the buyout of the pig farm and decided that an expert opinion to determine the price would be drawn up.
According to the Minister of Agriculture, the government will then rebuild.
According to Culture Minister Herman, "there are some numbers, but I'd probably thwart those intentions if I went public with them."
We'll see what the expert opinion brings.
According to some unofficial estimators, it could be hundreds of millions, according to others the price can reach up to a billion.
Either way, it won't be a cheap thing in any way.
At the same time, only a few hundred meters from the former camp there is a commemoration that the state invested almost ten million crowns in 2010.
Even if the entire transaction was financed by the UN Committee on Human Rights, which is probably not imminent, I think it would still be a waste of money.
Doesn't the government have other priorities?
The commemoration, a dignified remembrance place, stands by the will of history practically in the immediate vicinity of the tragedy and anyone who wants to be worshipping the memory of the suffering and murdered.
I don't see any reason to change that!
Let's leave it the way it is, and keep the remembrance site with the utmost diligence.
A responsible person, in this case the Government of the Czech Republic should treat the entrusted assets, i.e. the taxpayer's money, with the care of a proper manager.
At a minimum, as in your own company!
Of course, the Treasury and Deputy Prime Minister isn't an entire government.
But even so, from the point of view of the foregoing facts, I perceive the slogan of the ANO leader only as an empty pre-election proclamation.
In May this year I went straight to the place.
A very decent driving path, groomed parking, replicas of wooden camp barracks.
In one exposition, in the other integrated toilet with flowing water.
A small natural amphitee with the possibility of sitting and with a view of its own remembrance site, which is made of stones located in a slight slope.
Plenty flowers and wreaths.
All the attributes corresponding to the importance of this place, as befits and belongs.
An archaeological excaustration is currently taking place on the site of the original camp.
It would be pruddent to consider whether, for example, it would not be possible to preclude a part of the uncovered foundations and at least visually made available in an Appropriate manner.
Culture Minister Daniel Herman could tense in that direction.
In the event that he manages to enforce the purchase of the pig farm in the government for state money, it will be just another unfortunate step in his ever-quickly troubled tenure as the Minister of Culture.
Red Meat, Dairy Products and Boy May Alleviate Symptoms of Inflammable Intestinal Intestinal Distinction (IBD)
For your dizziness system, red meat and dairy products can be a nightmare.
However, a recurrence: a diet rich in animal protein and dairy products could help relieve symptoms of inflammatory bowel diseases.
Untreasonable disease, which affects up to 1.3 million U.S. residents and 300 000 patients in Great Britain, causes abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea, weight loss and extreme fatigue.
Patients are usually reminded not to ingest meat and dairy products to alleviate their symptoms. However, researchers at the University of Washington have found that an essential tryptophan amino acid contained in the protein helps to form immune cells that promote bowel resistance.
The head of the study Dr. Marco Colonna explained that immune cells protect the intestines and ensure the breakdown of harmful microbes from food.
Inflammatory entanglement cells are balanced by the number of cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without damaging sensitive tissue.
As soon as sausages tilt against the side of inflammation, inflammatory bowel disease can occur.
As part of the research, the researchers found the presence of immune cells supporting the body's tolerance in a mouse's stomach with a specific bacteria.
This bacterium requires the presence of tryptophan - one of the building blocks of the proteins to form cells.
Professor Colonna said: "We have found a connection between one kind of bacterium - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a common component of an intestinal microbiome and the development of cells that promote the body's tolerance.
The more tryptophan the mice had in their diet, the more they had these immune cells.”
At the same time, he has suggested that if it turns out that the same is also true for humans, then the combination of L. reuteri and tryptophan-protective diet would help to develop a more tolerable and less inflammatory intestinal intestinal environment.
Postgraduate researcher Dr. Luisa Cervantes-Barraganwas, as part of the study of a kind of immune-promoting body-promoting tolerance, discovered that one group of test mice had these cells, while the other group of the same mice breeled separately lacked cells.
She deduced from the fact that one group of mice has a different intestinal microbiome - a communion of bacterium, viruses and fungi, which normally dwell in the gastrointestinal tract.
Mice intestinals have been weathered using DNA alignment method, and researchers found six kinds of bacteria in mice with immune cells, which was lacked by a second group of mice.
Mice that were breged in a sterile environment and lacked an intestinal microbiome - so they didn't develop this kind of immune cells - was administered by the bacterium L. reuteri - and they formed immune cells.
To comprehensible how bacteria affects the immune system, the researchers culminated L. reuteri in the liquid and then transferred a small amount of fluid - without bacteria - undeveloped immune cells that have been stored in isolation.
Immune cells have evolved into tolerant cells.
After the removal of the active ingredient from the fluid, it turned out to be a by-product of tryptophane metabolism known as indolyl-3-acetic acid.
When researchers double the amount of tryptophane in the mice, the amount of these cells increased by 50 per cent. At the same time, the number of cells fell by half.
Dr. Cervantes-Barragan explained that humans have the same tolerant cells as mice, and most of us store L. reuteri bacteria in their gastrointestinal tract.
It is not known if tryptophane by-products from the bacterium L. reuteri cause the development of cells in humans in the same way as mice, but defects in tryptophane-related genes have been detected in people with inflammatory bowel disease.
Dr. Cervantes-Barragan added: "Each of us should probably strive for the development of these cells because they control the entrenoxiability of the inner surface of the intestines.
Potentially high level of tryptophane in the presence of the bacterium L. reuteri can increase the population of cells.”
