White House Props Cost Taxpayers Thousand Thousands of Dollars
The White House's decision to flock with a nautical helicopter at South Lawn on the occasion of the celebration of the U.S. manufacturing industry, which took place last month, came at $24,000.
The green-white Sikorsky VH-3D helicopter, also called Marine One in the presence of the president on board, was the culmination of a July 17 program in the White House that was a parade of U.S. design programs.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence and several senior White House officials have looked at a product parade from all 50 states.
Helicopter from Connecticut was exhibited next to a Yacht from Maine, a Wisconsin fire truck and a forkliftlift truck from Mississippi.
A personnel officer of the White House handed down a fragmentary order or FRAGO and applied for a helicopote presence less than 36 hours prior to the landing of a helicopter on South Lawn as part of an "extraordinary" event, Marine Helicopter Squadron One (HMX-1) records said.
"I wanted to make sure that you were aware of such a significant event," HMX-1 commander wrote in an e-mail to his supervisor, a representative of the aviation navy commander the day before the start of the event.
The report indicated that the helicopter will arrive at South Lawn on July 17 at approximately 7:00 a.m. on the occasion of the parade, which is scheduled to begin at 15:00 and will remain in place until the full evacuation site, which was planned between 18:00 and 21:00.
The e-mail further stated that if there was a delay on departures from the White House, a replacement crew will be dispatched for a return flight due to working time regulations.
The original garrrender remained in place that evening along with the helicopter, along with a unit of security personnel.
It wasn’t apparent from the records – which was obtained by TIME’s the weekly TIME if the second crew was eventually dispatched as well.
The Hatch Act prevents state officials from exploiting government assets for political purposes.
The records indicate that HMX-1 is investigating a possible violation of this regulation.
"We were wondering if it was a violation of the Hatch Act and we've been reassumed that everything was fine," the commander wrote.
"It sounds as if you've been blocked," the deputy commander wrote.
This "Made in America" parade has been marked as an official Event by the White House, so the Hatch Act wouldn't apply.
Official events also have political overtones.
In his participation in the event, the president promoted a medical reform that passed through the senate and talked about an attempt to resume a government order.
The use of aeronautical vehicles, such as Marine One or Air Force One for political purposes, is hardly a new phenomenon.
The presidents use them when traveling.
However, their presence purely because of presentation reasons is unusual.
Trump ruled control in February after a presidential jersey was in the background during his first rally as part of a campaign in Melbourne in Florida. His Twitter account is overwhelmed by complaints about the use of the presidential plane by his predecessors.
“White House is delighted with the ‘Made in America’ parade, which has unveiled products from all 50 states produced by Americans from U.S. materials,” press secretary representative Lindsay Walters said in a statement.
HMX-1 helicopter crews often train landings in the area of D.D. and South Lawn, reiteterorating eventual arrivals with the U.S. president of the White House with maximum accuracy.
While Trump took part in a G-20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 carried out 58 training landings at the White House.
The flight to the Made in America show was part of the crew's up-to-to-date training session, the state official said.
Helicopter unit on the same day aired another eleven hours as part of “non-presidential support missions”.
The White House said no additional props have been reimbursed by taxpayers' money.
Sikorsky spokeswoman stated that the company was not in any way involved in the negotiations to expose the helicopter to South Lawn.
HMX-1 does not retain the records of the budget for presidential flights, and the exact costs related to the parade are, according to the navy, it is difficult to determine.
According to the Ministry of Defense, the hourly price for the fiscal year 2017, the refund of DoD expenses for VH-3D is $24 380 per flight hour.
Repliminary footage gives a 30-minute time from Marine Corps Air Facility Quantico in Virginia and back.
The White House argues that the actual operating costs of the helicopter move considerably below that amount.
“It is very misleading the costs of taxpayers based on estimates for reimbursement of expenses,” Walters said.
"These estimations include personnel, maintenance and many other hidden costs included in annual costs."
According to RAND study 2015, DoD’s reimbursement doesn’t include personnel costs, but includes fuel prices, maintenance and contractual costs.
The White House press secretary Sean Spicer defended the use of a haycopter for parade purposes.
"The idea of a parade that takes place this week is to introduce products produced in the U.S.," Spicer said.
"I am aware that Sikorsky and Connecticut are very proud of the fact that they contribute to the defense of our state and that they provide the work for hundreds, if not thousands of people.
I think, like most Americans, that we are proud of the helicopter and the other military facilities in the production of which countless U.S. citizens are involved.
It’s important to be reminded.”
It's unclear if Trump had also looked at the helicopoeter during his South Lawn strudel. With certainty, it can only be said that he sanctued behind the wheel of one of the fire trucks.
Back in the White House, Trump addressed Sikorsky representatives and joking with the media about his own product fleet.
"Sikorsky I know very well," the president said, "I own three of them himself."
