Police Dogs Trained by Helicopter Overfliers in Most
Dogs specialized in the search for explosives and search dogs re-exerted today at Lake Most by helicopter.
The police dog must not be sermured and be afraid of flying, Petr Tomášek, head of police cynologists of the Ústí nad Labem Region, said.
The training of a police dog lasts about two years.
At the Bridge practiced both adult-trained dogs and those who are still undergoing the training.
In addition to the standard training, in which they were supposed to reveal sharp samples of explosives around the lake and look for the lost figurant, all 12 animals had to get into a helicopter and fly over the lake.
The main purpose of the exercise is to test the dogs in the helicopter.
So that as soon as the helicopter sits down, the dog can go to work, so that he does not have a problem with the helicopter's journey.
"A helicopter sits down, the dog goes and searches immediately," Tomasek said.
A dog that is unsettled or unsuccessfully is not able to start working right away is eliminated.
"Mubies are usually left by dog handlers, for example, in the garden," Tomášek added.
The police have 76 dogs with various specializations in the Ústice region.
German shepherds predominate, but there is also an increase in Belgian shepherds.
The handler takes care of him from the puppy.
"He educates it himself, he prepares it for the service that the dog will do for us," Tomášek added.
The animal undergoes repetitive veterinary examinations, which can detect possible medical disabilities.
If the dog is healthy and annoying, it passes the first exams at 20 months.
"It gets into a sharp intervention around two and a half to three years," Tomášek added.
The length of the police service depends on the dog's state of health.
They usually serve within eight- to ten years of age.
They usually live with their dog handlebars.
