According to the U.N, Afghan democracy relies on women in politics
Elections can only be plausible if they are attended by all members of society, including women, a U.N. representative in Afghanistan said on Wednesday.
Nicholas Hayshom, a senior U.N. Auxiliary Mission official, said hundreds of women are taking part in local council elections, three women are bidding for vice president, and more than 30 per cent of newly-registered electrors are women.
“The role of women cannot be disrespected,” he said.
Elections can be fully representative and trustworthiness only if women, as well as all related processes that are related to the election, take part.
Local and presidential elections in Afghanistan take place on April 5.
Taliban has published several threats against an electoral process, which is shifting international combat activities to the other side.
In this year's elections, for the first time, one democratically elected government is handing its power peacefully to the next government.
Haysom has stated that the strong role of women will strengthen Afghanistan's democracy.
An Affhalous Independent Electoral Apparel Commission said out of a total 3.6-million-out registered electorate, 1.2-million-dollar are women.
A unique brewery is brewing beer again.
For a good beer in a resurrected brewery.
Enthusiasm and honestifulness of the new landlord was returned to life a few years ago by the brewery in Lobč u Mšena.
Committed for the first time already in 1586 has a renaissance kernel and in the nineteenth century it was reconstructed into a vastro-profile operation.
This was followed by years of heyday, when the brewery was famous mainly for dark beers.
During the Second World War, production was halted and the brewery was nationalized in 1984.
Since then, the buildings have served the state farm.
The work of destruction was perfected by the owners of the brewery after 1989.
An abandoned area was devastated and disassembled into building materials.
In 2007, however, there was a new hope in the form of the new owners, the architects of Jana and Pavel Prouz.
They began to repair the buildings with the utmost respect for the spirit of the building and every detail.
They have been carefully devoted and engaged in the study of archival materials, trying to preserve the original materials and building elements.
The brewery takes the same form as in the nineteenth century, when its golden era took off.
Their work was appreciated by declaring the brewery as a cultural monument by the Ministry of Culture and the building also became a part of the European Industrial Heritage Geites.
In 2015, they festrously rebuild the tradition of brewing.
If you want to taste great Lobec beer in the picturesque landscape of Kokořínsko, please visit the bar in the brewery area.
When brewing beer, they use kokořín water springing in a protected landscape area, malt from Lobeč fields and Žatec hops.
In the bar you will taste traditional pale lager, but also dark beers and specials.
And don't miss a tour of the brewery, it takes place on Saturday and Sunday at noon and at half past two.
Largest Artillery Fortress in Czechoslovakia?
One of the world-leading forts on a global scale?
Such a unique building should be the fortress Stachelberg between Trutnov and Žacléř, at the height of an off-secret ridge above the Babí village.
It was originally supposed to consist of up to twelve bunkers connected by an extensive underground.
There was supposed to be a headquarters, barracks, ammunition warehouses, materials, a power plant, an engine room and a hospital.
Construction was terminated by the Munich Agreement.
In less than a year he managed to build a single mighty reinforced-concrete bunker and underground spaces were broken.
Thanks to enthusiasts from the Stachelberg Association, who in the early nineties began to reconstruct a unique building, you can also look here.
Subterrestrial is already 75 per cent accessible!
In Addition to an infantry cabin T-S 73 with an exposition and an under-the-reater, there is also an armed object of light fortifications, as well as two entangled infantry log cabins, trenches and an educational trail Forty Trutnov.
Refreshments, cosy gazebo and breathtaking views of the countryside guaranteed!
This unique and exquiserved Czech building has reached a list alongside such monuments such as the Great Wall of China, Traian's harbour near Rome or the Assyrian palace in Nineve and Nimrud.
Chotěšov Monastery originally from the beginning of the thirteenth-century, which today consists mainly of a massive four-winged convent building over a beautiful garden.
Unfortunately, the list that he has reached belongs to unique buildings that are at risk of extinction.
Enthusiasts don’t want to give up and organize a lot of events to support the monastery.
One of them is another Evening for the Chotěšov Monastery this Saturday 5th of August, where Vladimír Mišík, Ivan Hlas will perform, among others, Vladimír Mišík, you will see fencing performances and you will look at the places of a beautiful sprawling building, which are usually inaccessible.
Most importantly, you will enjoy the atmosphere of this great place.
Do you want to glimpse the Neanderthals in the face?
The exhibition Welcome to the Neanderthals in the Anthropos pavilion of the Moravian Provincial Museum in Brno encloses an unparalleled excursion to the deepest prehistory in the Stone Age, the world of our predecessors.
You will figure out how Neanderthals were perfectly able to cope with various extreme cylindrical climinal conditions, especially the alternation of Ice age and intercendigs.
In spite of their simple material culture, predators of their time.
You will also learn what they looked like, what their an anatomy, technological skills and hunting skills, but also their aesthetic sensibilities.
You wouldn't say that to the Neanderthals?
Let yourself be persuaded in Brno.
KELT & WORLD MUSIC festival takes place on Saturday the Fifth at Veveří Castle.
In Addition to the great music, you will see the building of a Celtic village, you will try a Celtic way of life including crafts, games for children and adults, divisulation, magic or ritual body painting.
La Quadrilla with Irish and scotch dances, bubonic jammings, bagpipe games and an arching fire surprise performed by Carpe Noct.
How does Moravian-Silesian taste?
The seventh year of the well-familiar gastro-festival How Smacks the Moravian-Silesian region takes place on Saturday, August 5, 2017 in the area of the mountain spa Karlova Studánka.
Chef Miroslav Kalina will cook, you will taste culinary specialties and regional food, you will enjoy musical and dance performances, a rich program for children such as creative workshops, theatre, bouncy castle, Šmoulí competition path and, of course, a number of competitions.
Get to know the plant with one click.
Students will be helped with the creation of herbariums and ordinary plant lovers by a new free application Pl@ntNet.
When they are interested in what kind of plant they just came across a walk, this application will advise them.
The only thing they're going to have to do is take a picture of the plant, and the app itself recognizes what kind it's.
The new application, which is available for both iOS and Android, was unveiled on Tuesday by representatives of the CEEV Ethical and Environmental Education Center in Brati-Solvism Mountain Park as part of the Secret Life of the City project.
Pupils from 50 Czech and Slovak and Slovak schools were also involved in the project, taking photos with teachers in the application database and adding plants from their surroundings.
The newly created French application is free to download on the website The Secret Life of the City.
The main goal of the French programmers was that as many people as possible contribute to the application with their images, thus increasing the number of plants that the application can recognize.
"Already, children who keep a tablet or a mobile phone in their hands can go into the forest," said Ivana Poláčková, coordinator of this project.
On the basis of a sufficiently high-quality photograph of the bloom bloom, the leaf or fruit of the plant can recognize its name in a matter of seconds, which is then assigned under the photo in both Czech and Slovak.
The French allowed us to translate the application into Slovak and Czech into the project The Secret Life of the City.
Therefore, it is unwelcome so that after setting up an account to the application to contribute to the application with a picture of a plant that does not yet occur in the database.
"French will evaluate whether it is beneficial, and then they add it to the database," Poláčková said.
GMB concerns about investing in McVitie's factory in Glasgow
Trade unions are demanding reassurances from executives of the biscuit maker McVitie's about a long-term production commitment at a factory in Glasgow.
Scotland's GMB trade union said the company was silent for the time being about a possible investment in a Tollcross factory.
There are more than 500 employees working in the factory, who, among other products, as well as biscuits Hobnob and Rich Tea.
McVitie's owner, Pladis, said it has invested more than £5 million in the area in the past few previous years.
She added that the employees and representatives of the trade unions will "among the first informed" in the event of changes to the operation of the factory.
In a letter to the company's builder, the Scottish GMB trade union expressed their concern that McVitie's "doesn't get their promises of financing and modernizing production lines."
GMB's chief organizer Drew Duffy added: "Fact that we didn't get a clear answer from the builder regarding the company's needs and a future investment plan in Tollcross is very disturbing for our members.
Generations of many families have worked here, and employees come mostly from the local community and Glasgow, where work at this factory represents a critical pillar of decent employment in high unemployment and social separation.
In terms of work, salaries/beneficiality - and in spite of the outlook for Brexit, the rising cost of living and declining slow-manufactured, the eastern part of Glasgow needs a hard-prosing producer like McVitie's."
Pladis spokeswowoman said: "Building executive Tollcross only received a letter from GMB only yesterday and the company will issue a statement as soon as possible, as we value an ongoing commitment to employees and union representatives.
A regular meeting with GMB employees and GMB representatives in Tollcross took place two months ago, and at this meeting, a lot of problems were openly talked about.
As in all companies operating in extremely competitive and inseparable economic conditions, we evaluate our operation on a regular basis, which applies to all our operations so that we can continue to compete successfully for others.
"In recent years, we have invested £5 million in Tollcross, especially in production lines and streamlining operations.
We very much appreciate Tollcross's heritage and locality, and in the event of any changes in the operation of the local factory, employees and union representatives will be immediately informed."
